# ch06 Kosovo – NATO Intervention and Its Consequences
# =============================================================================================
H('ch06-st1', 'Operation Allied Force and the debate on humanitarian intervention',
  terms=[('Operation Allied Force', 'NATO’s 78-day air campaign against the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia from 24 March to 10 June 1999'),
         ('humanitarian intervention', 'the use of military force by states in another state, without its consent, to prevent or end mass atrocities'),
         ('Responsibility to Protect (R2P)', 'the principle, developed by the ICISS in 2001 and endorsed at the UN World Summit in 2005, that states must protect populations from atrocities and the international community may act if they fail'),
         ('“illegal but legitimate”', 'the conclusion of the Independent International Commission on Kosovo (2000) that NATO’s action lacked UN authorisation but was justified')],
  facts=[('On what date did NATO begin bombing Yugoslavia?', '24 March 1999', ['10 June 1999', '15 January 1999', '17 March 2004'], 'Operation Allied Force began on 24 March and ended on 10 June.'),
         ('Why did NATO act without a UN Security Council resolution authorising force?', 'Russia and China were expected to veto it', ['The UN had no Security Council in 1999', 'NATO was part of the UN', 'Serbia had requested intervention'],
          'Russia (an ally of Serbia) and China opposed intervention.'),
         ('Which building in Belgrade was mistakenly bombed by NATO on 7 May 1999, killing three journalists?', 'The Chinese embassy', ['The Russian embassy', 'The Serbian parliament', 'The UN office'], 'NATO blamed outdated maps; China protested strongly.'),
         ('Which British prime minister set out a “doctrine of the international community” in a speech in Chicago in April 1999?', 'Tony Blair', ['John Major', 'Margaret Thatcher', 'Gordon Brown'], 'Blair argued that intervention could be justified against genocide and oppression.')],
  why=[('Explain why NATO’s intervention in Kosovo was legally controversial.', 'The UN Charter permits force only in self-defence or with Security Council authorisation; NATO had neither, so critics saw it as a breach of international law.'),
       ('Explain why NATO relied on air power rather than ground troops.', 'Member governments feared casualties and lacked public support for a ground war; air power minimised NATO losses, though it limited the ability to stop expulsions quickly.'),
       ('Explain why Milošević agreed to NATO’s terms in June 1999.', 'Escalating attacks on infrastructure, the threat of a ground invasion, KLA pressure and loss of Russian support (Chernomyrdin joined Ahtisaari in presenting terms) left him isolated.')],
  contrast=[('the legality of the Kosovo intervention', 'its legitimacy', 'Legality concerns conformity with international law (UN Charter); legitimacy concerns moral justification. The Kosovo Commission judged it illegal but legitimate.')],
  misc=[('The NATO air campaign caused no civilian deaths.', 'Human Rights Watch estimated about 500 civilian deaths from NATO action, e.g. the Đakovica convoy (14 April) and the RTS building (23 April).'),
        ('NATO’s bombing immediately stopped the expulsion of Kosovo Albanians.', 'Expulsions accelerated during the first weeks of bombing; about 850,000 fled before the war ended.')],
  cases=[('the bombing of the Radio Television of Serbia (RTS) building on 23 April 1999', 'NATO missiles hit the state broadcaster in Belgrade, killing 16 employees.',
          'the debate on the conduct of humanitarian intervention', 'NATO called it a propaganda target; Amnesty International called it a war crime, showing tension between humanitarian aims and methods.'),
         ('Tony Blair’s Chicago speech of 22 April 1999', 'Blair proposed criteria for intervention: certainty of the case, exhaustion of diplomacy, feasible military operations, long-term commitment and national interests.',
          'the justification of humanitarian intervention', 'It articulated a new doctrine that sovereignty could be overridden by humanitarian need, foreshadowing R2P.')],
  src=[('This is a just war, based not on any territorial ambitions but on values. We cannot let the evil of ethnic cleansing stand. Acts of genocide can never be a purely internal matter.',
        'Tony Blair, British Prime Minister, speech to the Economic Club of Chicago, 22 April 1999 (adapted extract)',
        'The war is just because it defends values against ethnic cleansing, and genocide cannot be treated as an internal affair of a state.',
        'A key speech by a leading NATO leader during the campaign, showing the moral justification for intervention.',
        'It is political persuasion intended to win US support, possibly for ground troops; it omits the legal problems and NATO’s strategic interests.'),
       ('The NATO military intervention was illegal but legitimate. It was illegal because it did not receive prior approval from the UN Security Council. However, it was justified because all diplomatic avenues had been exhausted and it liberated the majority population of Kosovo from a long period of oppression.',
        'Independent International Commission on Kosovo (chaired by Richard Goldstone), The Kosovo Report, Oxford University Press, 2000 (adapted)',
        'NATO’s action broke international law but was morally justified because diplomacy had failed and it ended oppression.',
        'A considered report by an independent international commission of experts after the war.',
        'It was sponsored by the Swedish government and its members largely sympathetic to intervention; its “illegal but legitimate” formula is contested by those who stress sovereignty.')],
  cmp='Both justify the intervention on humanitarian grounds. Blair presents it as a just war based on values and does not discuss legality, whereas the Commission admits that it was illegal while arguing it was legitimate; Blair writes as a participant, the Commission as an independent evaluator.',
  ev=[('NATO’s intervention in Kosovo in 1999 was motivated primarily by humanitarian concerns.',
       ['ethnic cleansing and memory of Srebrenica (1995)', 'Blair’s doctrine of the international community', 'refugee crisis'],
       ['NATO credibility at its 50th anniversary', 'regional stability and refugee flows into Europe', 'US strategic interest in the Balkans'],
       'Humanitarian concerns were real and important, but intervention also served NATO’s credibility and European security interests.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was Operation Allied Force a successful humanitarian intervention?',
           ['ended Serbian rule and allowed refugee return', 'expulsions accelerated during bombing', 'civilian casualties and legality', 'reverse ethnic cleansing of Serbs and Roma after June 1999'],
           'It achieved its main aims but at significant humanitarian and legal cost, so success was partial.')],
)

H('ch06-st2', 'the international administration of Kosovo after 1999',
  terms=[('Resolution 1244', 'the UN Security Council resolution of 10 June 1999 that placed Kosovo under UN interim administration with an international security presence'),
         ('UNMIK', 'the United Nations Interim Administration Mission in Kosovo, which governed Kosovo from 1999'),
         ('KFOR', 'the NATO-led Kosovo Force, the international security presence deployed in June 1999'),
         ('“standards before status”', 'the UNMIK policy of 2002–2003 that Kosovo must meet standards of democracy and minority rights before its final status was decided')],
  facts=[('Who was the first head (Special Representative of the Secretary-General) of UNMIK?', 'Bernard Kouchner', ['Martti Ahtisaari', 'Kofi Annan', 'Richard Holbrooke'], 'Kouchner, French co-founder of Médecins Sans Frontières, led UNMIK in 1999–2001.'),
         ('Which country’s troops made a surprise dash to Pristina airport in June 1999?', 'Russia', ['China', 'France', 'Albania'], 'Russian troops from Bosnia arrived ahead of NATO on 12 June 1999.'),
         ('Under Resolution 1244, Kosovo formally remained part of which state?', 'The Federal Republic of Yugoslavia', ['Albania', 'North Macedonia', 'The European Union'], 'The resolution reaffirmed Yugoslav sovereignty and territorial integrity while granting substantial autonomy.'),
         ('In which year did serious anti-Serb riots occur across Kosovo, killing 19 people?', '2004', ['1999', '2008', '1989'], 'The riots of 17–18 March 2004 destroyed homes and Orthodox churches.')],
  why=[('Explain why Resolution 1244 left Kosovo’s final status unresolved.', 'Russia and China would accept a resolution only if it reaffirmed Yugoslav sovereignty; Western states wanted autonomy; ambiguity was the price of Security Council agreement.'),
       ('Explain why minorities in Kosovo were at risk after June 1999.', 'Returning Albanians took revenge on Serbs and Roma accused of collaboration; KFOR could not protect scattered communities, and about 200,000 non-Albanians left.'),
       ('Explain why the March 2004 riots damaged UNMIK’s reputation.', 'UNMIK and KFOR failed to protect Serb communities and churches, revealing the weakness of international administration and frustration among Albanians over status.')],
  contrast=[('UNMIK’s role', 'KFOR’s role', 'UNMIK was the civilian administration responsible for government, law and institution-building; KFOR was the NATO-led military force responsible for security and demilitarising the KLA.')],
  misc=[('Resolution 1244 recognised Kosovo as an independent state.', 'It placed Kosovo under UN interim administration and reaffirmed Yugoslav sovereignty; final status was left to a later process.'),
        ('After 1999 the conflict in Kosovo ended with no further ethnic violence.', 'Revenge attacks on Serbs and Roma, a divided Mitrovica and the 2004 riots show continued violence.')],
  cases=[('the Russian dash to Pristina airport on 12 June 1999', 'About 200 Russian troops seized the airport; NATO commander Wesley Clark ordered it blocked, but British General Mike Jackson refused, reportedly saying he would not start the Third World War.',
          'tensions between the great powers over Kosovo', 'The episode shows Russia’s determination to have a role and the risk of confrontation in the post-Cold War settlement.'),
         ('the divided city of Mitrovica', 'The Ibar river separated an Albanian south from a Serb-majority north that remained linked to Belgrade’s parallel structures.',
          'the limits of international administration', 'UNMIK could not integrate the north, which remained a flashpoint and challenge to Kosovo’s sovereignty.')],
  src=[('Decides on the deployment in Kosovo, under United Nations auspices, of international civil and security presences… Reaffirming the commitment of all Member States to the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia… substantial autonomy and meaningful self-administration for Kosovo.',
        'United Nations Security Council Resolution 1244, adopted 10 June 1999 (extracts)',
        'The UN will deploy international civil and security forces in Kosovo, and Kosovo will have substantial autonomy while Yugoslav sovereignty is respected.',
        'The official legal foundation of the post-war settlement, agreed by the Security Council including Russia and China.',
        'As a diplomatic compromise its wording is deliberately ambiguous and says nothing about how the arrangements worked in practice.'),
       ('In the first months after the war, the international mission arrived to find no administration, no police and no courts. Revenge attacks on Serbs went largely unpunished, and the UN was always one step behind.',
        'Paraphrased from Iain King and Whit Mason, former UNMIK officials, Peace at Any Price: How the World Failed Kosovo, 2006',
        'The UN mission was unprepared, could not maintain order and failed to stop revenge attacks against Serbs.',
        'Written by insiders with first-hand experience of UNMIK, it gives an informed account of its practical problems.',
        'Former officials may be seeking to explain or criticise decisions in which they were involved; the title shows a strongly critical thesis.')],
  cmp='Both concern the international administration of Kosovo. Resolution 1244 sets out the aims of civil and security presences and autonomy within Yugoslavia, while King and Mason describe the practical failure to provide order and protect Serbs; A sets out intentions, B outcomes.',
  ev=[('The international administration of Kosovo between 1999 and 2008 was a failure.',
       ['failed to protect minorities (2004 riots)', 'divided Mitrovica', 'slow economic development and corruption'],
       ['maintained peace between Serbia and Kosovo', 'built institutions and held elections from 2001', 'refugee return of Albanians'],
       'It prevented renewed war and built institutions but failed to protect minorities or resolve status.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the success of UNMIK and KFOR in Kosovo between 1999 and 2008.',
           ['security and demilitarisation of the KLA', 'elections and Constitutional Framework (2001)', 'minority protection failures', 'status unresolved until 2008'],
           'They achieved basic security and institutions but failed to create a stable multi-ethnic society.')],
)

H('ch06-st3', 'the long-term consequences of the Kosovo conflict',
  terms=[('ICTY', 'the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia, created by UN Security Council Resolution 827 in 1993 and based in The Hague'),
         ('Ahtisaari Plan', 'the 2007 proposal by UN envoy Martti Ahtisaari for supervised independence of Kosovo with strong minority protections'),
         ('Kosovo Specialist Chambers', 'a court in The Hague, established in 2015–2016 under Kosovo law, to try alleged crimes by KLA members'),
         ('Brussels Agreement', 'the EU-mediated agreement of April 2013 normalising some relations between Serbia and Kosovo, including arrangements for Serb municipalities')],
  facts=[('On what date did Kosovo declare independence?', '17 February 2008', ['10 June 1999', '28 June 1989', '22 July 2010'], 'The Kosovo assembly declared independence, which Serbia rejected.'),
         ('In 2010 the International Court of Justice ruled that Kosovo’s declaration of independence:', 'Did not violate international law', ['Was illegal and void', 'Required Serbian approval', 'Made Kosovo a UN member'],
          'The ICJ advisory opinion of 22 July 2010 found no violation of general international law or Resolution 1244.'),
         ('Why did Slobodan Milošević’s ICTY trial end without a verdict?', 'He died in detention in March 2006', ['He was acquitted in 2003', 'He was pardoned by Serbia', 'The ICTY was abolished'], 'He had been indicted in May 1999 and transferred to The Hague in 2001.'),
         ('Which former KLA leader and president of Kosovo was indicted by the Kosovo Specialist Chambers in 2020?', 'Hashim Thaçi', ['Ibrahim Rugova', 'Adem Jashari', 'Bernard Kouchner'], 'Thaçi resigned to face trial for war crimes and crimes against humanity.')],
  why=[('Explain why Kosovo has not become a member of the United Nations.', 'Serbia rejects its independence and Russia and China, as permanent Security Council members, would veto membership.'),
       ('Explain why the indictment of Milošević in May 1999 was significant.', 'He was the first sitting head of state indicted by an international tribunal for war crimes, showing that leaders could be held accountable.'),
       ('Explain why some states refused to recognise Kosovo’s independence.', 'States such as Spain, Russia and China feared the precedent for their own separatist regions and stressed territorial integrity and the lack of Security Council approval.')],
  contrast=[('the ICTY', 'the Kosovo Specialist Chambers', 'The ICTY was a UN tribunal (1993–2017) trying crimes across the former Yugoslavia, mostly by Serb leaders in the Kosovo cases; the Specialist Chambers is a Kosovo court sitting in The Hague to try crimes by KLA members.')],
  misc=[('All countries recognised Kosovo after the ICJ opinion of 2010.', 'Recognition remains divided: over 100 states recognise Kosovo but Serbia, Russia, China and five EU states, including Spain, do not.'),
        ('The ICTY prosecuted only Serbs for crimes in Kosovo.', 'It also tried KLA commanders such as Ramush Haradinaj and Fatmir Limaj, who were acquitted, which fuelled controversy about witness intimidation.')],
  cases=[('the ICTY judgment in Prosecutor v. Šainović et al. (2009)', 'Five senior Serbian and Yugoslav officials were convicted of deportation and murder in Kosovo; Milan Milutinović was acquitted.',
          'international accountability for crimes in Kosovo', 'It established judicially that a campaign of expulsion was carried out by state forces in 1999.'),
         ('the ICJ advisory opinion of July 2010', 'The court found that the declaration of independence did not violate international law, but did not rule on whether Kosovo was a state.',
          'the contested status of Kosovo', 'It strengthened Kosovo’s case but left recognition a political choice for states.')],
  src=[('Kosovo is a unique case that demands a unique solution. It does not create a precedent for other unresolved conflicts.',
        'Martti Ahtisaari, UN Special Envoy for Kosovo’s future status, report to the UN Security Council, March 2007 (adapted)',
        'Kosovo’s independence should be accepted because its circumstances are unique, so it would not set a precedent for other separatist conflicts.',
        'The official recommendation of the UN envoy who led status negotiations; it shows the reasoning behind supervised independence.',
        'Ahtisaari had concluded that negotiations had failed and wished to persuade the Council; the “unique case” argument is disputed, especially by Russia and Spain.'),
       ('By recognising the unilateral secession of Kosovo, the West has opened a Pandora’s box. Kosovo is a terrible precedent which will come back to hit them.',
        'Paraphrased from Vladimir Putin, President of Russia, statement in February 2008',
        'Recognition of Kosovo is a dangerous precedent for secession that will harm the West in the future.',
        'It shows the official Russian position at the moment of independence.',
        'It is a political statement reflecting Russia’s own interests and alliances (Serbia) and its opposition to NATO, not a neutral legal analysis.')],
  cmp='Both discuss whether Kosovo sets a precedent. Ahtisaari insists Kosovo is unique and sets no precedent, whereas Putin calls it a terrible precedent; one supports independence, the other opposes it.',
  ev=[('Kosovo’s declaration of independence in 2008 resolved the Kosovo question.',
       ['ICJ opinion 2010', 'over 100 recognitions', 'state institutions and constitution'],
       ['Serbia, Russia, China and five EU states do not recognise it', 'north Kosovo resists Pristina', 'no UN membership; tensions continue'],
       'Independence changed Kosovo’s status in practice but did not settle the question internationally.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did international justice achieve accountability for the Kosovo conflict?',
           ['Milošević indictment and death in 2006', 'Šainović et al. convictions', 'Haradinaj and Limaj acquittals', 'Kosovo Specialist Chambers and Thaçi trial'],
           'International justice established important facts and convictions but its record is incomplete and contested.')],
)

H('ch06-st4', 'why the international community responded differently in Rwanda and Kosovo',
  terms=[('selective intervention', 'the tendency of states to intervene in some humanitarian crises but not in others, according to interests and circumstances'),
         ('“CNN effect”', 'the theory that intensive media coverage of suffering pressures governments to intervene'),
         ('Srebrenica', 'the Bosnian town where Bosnian Serb forces killed about 8,000 Bosniak men and boys in July 1995, despite the presence of Dutch UN peacekeepers')],
  facts=[('Which of the following best explains NATO’s willingness to act in Kosovo but not in Rwanda?', 'Kosovo was in Europe, threatening NATO members’ security and NATO’s credibility',
          ['Rwanda had requested no help', 'More people died in Kosovo than in Rwanda', 'Kosovo had UN Security Council approval for action'], 'Strategic location and alliance credibility were central.'),
         ('Approximately how many people were killed in Rwanda in 1994 compared with Kosovo in 1998–1999?', 'About 500,000–800,000 in Rwanda; about 10,000–13,000 in Kosovo',
          ['About 10,000 in each', 'About 1 million in Kosovo; about 50,000 in Rwanda', 'About 13,000 in Rwanda; about 800,000 in Kosovo'], 'The scale of killing in Rwanda was far greater.')],
  why=[('Explain why the experience of Srebrenica influenced Western policy in Kosovo.', 'Western governments felt shame at failing to prevent the 1995 massacre and were determined not to allow another atrocity in Europe.'),
       ('Explain why the experience of Somalia influenced Western policy in Rwanda.', 'The deaths of US soldiers in Mogadishu in October 1993 made the USA unwilling to risk troops in another African conflict and led to PDD-25.')],
  contrast=[('the international response in Rwanda', 'the international response in Kosovo', 'In Rwanda the UN reduced its force and major powers refused to intervene during the genocide; in Kosovo NATO used force within months of the main atrocities, without UN authorisation.')],
  misc=[('The international community intervened in Kosovo because more people were killed there than in Rwanda.', 'Far more were killed in Rwanda; Kosovo’s location in Europe, NATO credibility and refugee flows explain the different response.'),
        ('Racism is the only reason for the lack of intervention in Rwanda.', 'Many historians see racial and Eurocentric attitudes as a factor, but post-Somalia policy, lack of strategic interest and the speed of the genocide were also important.')],
  cases=[('the refugee crisis in Macedonia and Albania in spring 1999', 'Hundreds of thousands of Kosovo Albanians fled into neighbouring states, threatening regional stability and reaching Western Europe.',
          'the role of interests in shaping intervention', 'Refugee flows into Europe gave Western states a direct interest in Kosovo, absent in Rwanda.')],
  src=[('The international community, Africa included, must bear its share of responsibility for this tragedy. We could have done more to stop what happened in Rwanda. The world must not be indifferent again.',
        'Paraphrased from Bill Clinton, US President, speech at Kigali airport, 25 March 1998',
        'The international community, including the USA, failed Rwanda and should have done more.',
        'An official acknowledgement by the US President, showing a change in the US position four years after the genocide.',
        'It is a carefully worded public statement that spreads responsibility and implies ignorance (“we did not fully appreciate”), concealing deliberate US policy choices in 1994.'),
       ('If we do not act in Kosovo, the credibility of NATO, the cornerstone of European security for fifty years, will be destroyed. And we will face a flood of refugees across the borders of our allies.',
        'Paraphrased from statements by US Secretary of State Madeleine Albright, March 1999',
        'Intervention in Kosovo is necessary to protect NATO’s credibility and to prevent refugee flows into allied countries.',
        'It reveals strategic reasons given by a key US policy-maker for intervention in Kosovo.',
        'It is a public argument intended to build support for action; it emphasises interests and omits doubts about legality.')],
  cmp='Both speak of Western responsibility. Clinton apologises for inaction in Rwanda, whereas Albright argues for action in Kosovo on grounds of NATO credibility and refugees; together they suggest that interests, not only humanitarian need, explain the different responses.',
  ev=[('Strategic interests, rather than humanitarian concerns, explain why the international community intervened in Kosovo but not in Rwanda.',
       ['European location and NATO credibility', 'refugee flows into Europe', 'no strategic interest in Rwanda after Somalia'],
       ['lessons of Srebrenica and Rwanda itself encouraged action in 1999', 'Rwanda’s speed and media framing as “tribal”', 'different institutions: UN in Rwanda, NATO in Kosovo'],
       'Strategic interests were decisive, but changing humanitarian norms after Rwanda and Srebrenica also mattered.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the international response to the conflicts in Rwanda (1994) and Kosovo (1999).',
           ['UN reduced force vs NATO intervention', 'role of USA in each case', 'media and public opinion', 'legality: UN mandate vs no authorisation', 'consequences: tribunals in both'],
           'The responses differed mainly because of strategic interest and location, though both produced international tribunals.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch07 Authoritarian Rule – Conditions for the Emergence of Authoritarian States
# =============================================================================================
H('ch07-st1', 'the economic conditions that helped authoritarian leaders gain power',
  terms=[('hyperinflation', 'extremely rapid inflation that destroys the value of money, as in Germany in 1923 when a US dollar was worth over 4 trillion marks'),
         ('Great Depression', 'the world economic slump that followed the Wall Street Crash of October 1929, causing mass unemployment and the collapse of trade'),
         ('autarky', 'economic self-sufficiency, an aim of Nazi and Fascist economic policy'),
         ('Article 48', 'the clause of the Weimar Constitution that allowed the president to rule by emergency decree, used heavily by Chancellor Brüning in 1930–1932')],
  facts=[('In which year did hyperinflation reach its peak in Germany?', '1923', ['1919', '1929', '1933'], 'The French and Belgian occupation of the Ruhr in January 1923 and passive resistance accelerated inflation.'),
         ('Approximately how many Germans were registered unemployed in early 1932?', 'About 6 million', ['About 600,000', 'About 1 million', 'About 20 million'], 'Registered unemployment peaked at over 6 million in early 1932.'),
         ('What share of the vote did the Nazi Party win in the Reichstag election of July 1932?', 'About 37 per cent', ['About 2.6 per cent', 'About 18 per cent', 'About 52 per cent'], 'They won 37.3%, becoming the largest party; in 1928 they had won only 2.6%.'),
         ('Which of the following describes the “biennio rosso” in Italy?', 'Two years of strikes, factory occupations and land seizures in 1919–1920', ['Mussolini’s first two years in power', 'The two years of the Abyssinian war', 'The period of hyperinflation in 1923'],
          'Fear of socialist revolution drove landowners and industrialists towards Fascism.')],
  why=[('Explain why the Great Depression increased support for the Nazi Party.', 'Mass unemployment, business failures and farm debt discredited the Weimar parties; the Nazis offered work, national revival and a scapegoat, and their vote rose from 18% (1930) to 37% (1932).'),
       ('Explain why the middle classes were particularly affected by economic crisis in Weimar Germany.', 'Hyperinflation in 1923 wiped out savings and pensions, and the Depression threatened shopkeepers, farmers and white-collar workers, who turned to the Nazis as protection against both big business and communism.'),
       ('Explain why economic crisis weakened the Guomindang in China before 1949.', 'Hyperinflation after 1945, corruption and wartime destruction alienated urban and middle-class support, while the CCP offered land reform to peasants.')],
  contrast=[('the impact of hyperinflation in 1923', 'the impact of the Depression after 1929', 'Hyperinflation ruined savers but the Republic survived, helped by the Dawes Plan; the Depression caused mass unemployment and political polarisation that brought the Nazis to power.')],
  misc=[('Hitler came to power because the Nazis won a majority of votes in a free election.', 'The Nazis never won a majority in a free election (37.3% at their peak); Hitler was appointed chancellor by Hindenburg on 30 January 1933 after backroom intrigue.'),
        ('Economic crisis automatically produces authoritarian rule.', 'Britain and the USA suffered the Depression but remained democracies; economic crisis contributed where political systems were weak and elites were willing to accept dictators.')],
  cases=[('Chancellor Heinrich Brüning’s deflationary policy (1930–1932)', 'Brüning cut wages, benefits and government spending by presidential decree under Article 48.',
          'how economic policy undermined democracy', 'Austerity deepened unemployment and accustomed Germans to government without the Reichstag, weakening parliamentary democracy before Hitler.'),
         ('the occupation of factories in northern Italy in September 1920', 'About 400,000 metalworkers occupied factories in Milan and Turin before the movement collapsed.',
          'the appeal of strong leadership to economic elites', 'Fear of socialist revolution led industrialists and landowners to fund Fascist squads that attacked unions and socialists.')],
  src=[('[Poster] A crowd of grey, exhausted unemployed men and women with downcast faces stands above the slogan: “Unsere letzte Hoffnung: Hitler” (Our last hope: Hitler).',
        'Nazi election poster designed by Hans Schweitzer (“Mjölnir”) for the presidential election of 1932 (description)',
        'Hitler is the only hope for ordinary Germans suffering from unemployment and despair in the Depression.',
        'An official Nazi poster from the height of the Depression, showing how the party exploited economic suffering.',
        'It is propaganda, so it shows the Nazis’ message, not how voters actually responded or why people voted Nazi.'),
       ('Unemployment alone does not explain the Nazi vote: the unemployed tended to vote Communist. The Nazis drew disproportionately on Protestant, rural and middle-class voters who feared the collapse of their status.',
        'Paraphrased from Richard J. Evans, British historian, The Coming of the Third Reich, 2003, drawing on electoral studies by Jürgen Falter',
        'The Nazi vote came mostly from middle-class, Protestant and rural voters fearing loss of status rather than from the unemployed, who tended to support the KPD.',
        'A leading historian of Nazi Germany using statistical electoral research; gives a detailed, evidence-based interpretation.',
        'It is a synthesis of other research, and electoral statistics cannot reveal individual motives.')],
  cmp='Both link Nazi support to the Depression. The poster claims the unemployed see Hitler as their only hope, whereas Evans argues that the unemployed tended to vote Communist and Nazi support came mainly from the anxious middle classes; A is propaganda, B analysis.',
  ev=[('Economic crisis was the most important reason for the emergence of authoritarian states in the inter-war period.',
       ['Depression and Nazi vote rise 1930–1932', 'biennio rosso and Fascist support', 'GMD hyperinflation 1947–1949'],
       ['weak democratic institutions (PR, Article 48)', 'impact of war and the Versailles settlement', 'role of elites (Hindenburg, Victor Emmanuel III)', 'ideological appeal and leadership'],
       'Economic crisis was a powerful catalyst, but authoritarian leaders came to power only where political systems were weak and elites collaborated.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the role of economic factors in the rise to power of two authoritarian leaders, each from a different region.',
           ['Hitler: Depression and unemployment', 'Mussolini: post-war inflation and biennio rosso', 'Mao: GMD hyperinflation and peasant poverty', 'Castro: sugar dependence and inequality'],
           'Economic crisis aided each rise, but its importance differed according to political context.')],
)

H('ch07-st2', 'political weakness, social division and war as conditions for authoritarian rule',
  terms=[('“stab-in-the-back” myth', 'the false claim that the German army had not been defeated in 1918 but betrayed by socialists, Jews and politicians at home'),
         ('“mutilated victory”', 'the nationalist complaint that Italy was cheated of territory promised by the Treaty of London (1915) at the Paris Peace Conference'),
         ('proportional representation', 'an electoral system giving parties seats in proportion to votes, which in Weimar Germany produced many parties and unstable coalitions'),
         ('warlord era', 'the period 1916–1928 in China when regional military leaders controlled much of the country after the death of Yuan Shikai')],
  facts=[('Which poet seized the city of Fiume in 1919, defying the Italian government?', 'Gabriele D’Annunzio', ['Filippo Marinetti', 'Giovanni Gentile', 'Gabriele Rossetti'], 'D’Annunzio held Fiume until December 1920, showing the weakness of liberal Italy.'),
         ('How many governments did Weimar Germany have between 1919 and 1933?', 'About 20', ['3', '8', '50'], 'Coalitions changed frequently; the average government lasted less than a year.'),
         ('Which of the following weakened the Guomindang regime most during 1937–1945?', 'The war against Japan', ['The Long March', 'The Cultural Revolution', 'The Korean War'], 'Japan occupied eastern China, forcing the GMD to Chongqing and destroying its urban base.')],
  why=[('Explain why the First World War helped create conditions for the rise of Fascism in Italy.', 'War caused debt, inflation and 650,000 deaths; the “mutilated victory” angered nationalists; demobilised soldiers joined squads; liberal governments seemed weak and divided.'),
       ('Explain why the Weimar Constitution was vulnerable to authoritarian challenge.', 'Proportional representation produced fragmented coalitions, Article 48 allowed rule by decree, and elites in the army, judiciary and civil service remained hostile to the Republic.'),
       ('Explain why the Japanese invasion helped the CCP in China.', 'The CCP led guerrilla resistance behind Japanese lines, presenting itself as patriotic and expanding its base areas, while the GMD bore the brunt of conventional fighting and lost credibility.')],
  contrast=[('the weakness of liberal Italy', 'the weakness of the Weimar Republic', 'Liberal Italy was an elite-dominated system weakened by trasformismo and limited legitimacy; Weimar was a new democracy weakened by its association with defeat and Versailles, PR and Article 48.')],
  misc=[('The Weimar Republic was doomed from the beginning.', 'It survived crises in 1919–1923 and stabilised in 1924–1929 under Stresemann; it was the Depression and elite decisions that destroyed it.'),
        ('Mussolini seized power through a military victory over the Italian army.', 'The March on Rome was a bluff; King Victor Emmanuel III refused to sign a decree of martial law and appointed Mussolini prime minister.')],
  cases=[('the Treaty of Versailles (June 1919)', 'Germany lost 13% of its territory, its colonies and its armed forces were limited to 100,000 men; Article 231 assigned war guilt.',
          'the impact of war on the rise of authoritarian movements', 'Nationalist resentment of the “Diktat” and the “November criminals” was exploited by the Nazis throughout the 1920s.'),
         ('the May Fourth Movement of 1919', 'Students in Beijing protested against the Versailles decision to give Germany’s Shandong rights to Japan.',
          'the link between national humiliation and new political movements', 'It stimulated radical nationalism and Marxism, leading to the founding of the CCP in 1921.')],
  src=[('The army was not defeated in the field; it was stabbed in the back. Where the blame lies is clearly proved. If any further proof were needed, it lies in the statement of the British general that the German army was stabbed in the back.',
        'Paraphrased from Paul von Hindenburg, former Chief of the General Staff, testimony to a Reichstag committee of inquiry, 18 November 1919',
        'Germany’s army was not defeated militarily but betrayed by people at home.',
        'Testimony from the most famous German commander shows how the myth was given authority at an early date.',
        'Hindenburg sought to deflect responsibility for defeat from the army and himself; the claim was false and served political purposes.'),
       ('It was not the strength of fascism but the weakness of the liberal state that made Mussolini’s success possible. The liberal elites thought they could use him to restore order and then discard him.',
        'Paraphrased from Denis Mack Smith, British historian, Mussolini, 1981',
        'Mussolini succeeded because the liberal state and its elites were weak and believed they could control him.',
        'A leading historian of Italy with wide knowledge of sources; offers a clear interpretation of the conditions for Fascism.',
        'Mack Smith is known for a critical view of Mussolini; as an interpretation it may underestimate Fascism’s genuine support.')],
  cmp='Both show elites contributing to the rise of authoritarianism. Hindenburg himself spreads a myth that undermined the Weimar Republic, while Mack Smith analyses how liberal elites helped Mussolini; A is an example of elite behaviour, B an explanation of it.',
  ev=[('The weakness of democratic systems was more important than economic crisis in the rise of authoritarian states.',
       ['Weimar PR and Article 48', 'liberal Italy’s trasformismo and the king’s decision', 'warlord era China'],
       ['Depression drove Nazi vote', 'biennio rosso fear of socialism', 'GMD hyperinflation'],
       'Weak systems were a necessary condition and economic crisis the catalyst; the two worked together.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was the impact of war the main condition for the emergence of authoritarian states?',
           ['WWI and Fascism, Versailles and Nazism', 'Sino-Japanese war and the CCP', 'economic crisis', 'political weakness'],
           'War created many of the conditions but authoritarian leaders came to power through the interaction of war with economic and political crises.')],
)

H('ch07-st3', 'the ideologies of fascism, Nazism and communism',
  terms=[('fascism', 'an ultra-nationalist ideology that glorifies the state, action and violence, rejects liberalism and Marxism and seeks national rebirth under a single leader'),
         ('Volksgemeinschaft', 'the Nazi ideal of a racially pure “people’s community” united above class and party'),
         ('Lebensraum', 'living space; Hitler’s aim of conquering territory in eastern Europe for German settlement'),
         ('totalitarianism', 'a system in which a single party seeks total control over all aspects of public and private life, as defined by Friedrich and Brzezinski in 1956'),
         ('Maoism', 'Mao Zedong’s adaptation of Marxism-Leninism to China, emphasising the revolutionary role of the peasantry, the mass line and continuous revolution')],
  facts=[('Who wrote “Mein Kampf”, published in 1925–1926?', 'Adolf Hitler', ['Joseph Goebbels', 'Alfred Rosenberg', 'Heinrich Himmler'], 'Hitler dictated the first volume in Landsberg prison after the Beer Hall Putsch.'),
         ('Which political philosopher co-wrote “The Doctrine of Fascism” (1932) with Mussolini?', 'Giovanni Gentile', ['Antonio Gramsci', 'Benedetto Croce', 'Gabriele D’Annunzio'], 'Gentile was Fascism’s leading philosopher.'),
         ('Which of the following was central to Mao’s adaptation of Marxism?', 'The peasantry as the main revolutionary force', ['The urban proletariat as the only revolutionary class', 'Racial purity', 'Restoration of the monarchy'], 'Mao’s 1927 Hunan Report stressed the peasants’ revolutionary potential.'),
         ('Which author published “The Origins of Totalitarianism” in 1951?', 'Hannah Arendt', ['Ian Kershaw', 'Hans Mommsen', 'Mahmood Mamdani'], 'Arendt compared Nazi Germany and the Stalinist USSR as totalitarian systems.')],
  why=[('Explain why racial ideology distinguished Nazism from Italian Fascism.', 'Nazism placed biological racism and antisemitism at its core, aiming at a racial state and Lebensraum; Italian Fascism centred on the state and nation and adopted race laws only in 1938.'),
       ('Explain why communist and fascist regimes are sometimes grouped together as totalitarian.', 'Both used a single party, a leader cult, terror, control of media and the economy and an official ideology demanding mass mobilisation.'),
       ('Explain why fascist ideology appealed to many people after the First World War.', 'It promised national rebirth, order against communism, and a role for veterans, offering an emotional, action-based alternative to discredited liberal politics.')],
  contrast=[('fascist ideology', 'communist ideology', 'Fascism exalted the nation and state, accepted private property and rejected class struggle; communism aimed at a classless, internationalist society through abolition of private property.'),
            ('Italian Fascism', 'German Nazism', 'Both were ultra-nationalist and anti-communist, but Nazism made racial antisemitism and Lebensraum central, while Fascism focused on the state and the corporate economy.')],
  misc=[('Fascism and communism were essentially the same ideology.', 'They used similar methods, but their aims differed fundamentally: fascism defended nation and hierarchy and private property, communism sought a classless society.'),
        ('Mao applied Marxism exactly as Marx had described it.', 'Marx expected revolution in industrial societies led by the proletariat; Mao adapted it to rural China, relying on peasant armies and guerrilla war.')],
  cases=[('the Fascist slogan “everything within the state, nothing outside the state, nothing against the state” (Mussolini, 1925)', 'Mussolini used it in a speech at La Scala in October 1925 to define the Fascist state.',
          'the totalitarian aspirations of fascism', 'It shows Fascism’s claim to absorb all of society into the state, though in practice the monarchy and Church kept independence.'),
         ('Mao’s Report on an Investigation of the Peasant Movement in Hunan (1927)', 'Mao argued that the peasants would rise “like a mighty storm” and sweep away the old order.',
          'the distinctive character of Maoist ideology', 'It shifted the revolutionary base from cities to countryside, the core of Mao’s strategy.')],
  src=[('Anti-individualistic, the Fascist conception of life stresses the importance of the State and accepts the individual only in so far as his interests coincide with those of the State. For the Fascist, everything is in the State, and nothing human or spiritual exists, much less has value, outside the State.',
        'Benito Mussolini and Giovanni Gentile, “The Doctrine of Fascism”, Enciclopedia Italiana, 1932 (extract, translation)',
        'Fascism places the state above the individual; individuals have value only in so far as they serve the state.',
        'An official statement of Fascist ideology by the leader and his chief philosopher.',
        'Written ten years after Mussolini took power, it presents a coherent theory that may rationalise policies that were in practice opportunistic.'),
       ('A revolution is not a dinner party. A revolution is an insurrection, an act of violence by which one class overthrows another.',
        'Mao Zedong, Report on an Investigation of the Peasant Movement in Hunan, March 1927 (extract, translation)',
        'Revolution requires violence by one class against another; it cannot be gentle.',
        'Written by Mao early in his career, it shows his commitment to class struggle and violent revolution.',
        'It is a party document intended to persuade CCP leaders to support peasant radicalism; it is not a full statement of later Maoism.')],
  cmp='Both reject liberal individualism. Mussolini stresses the supremacy of the state over the individual, while Mao stresses class struggle and violence by one class against another; Fascism focuses on national unity, Maoism on class conflict.',
  ev=[('Ideology was more important than economic crisis in the rise of authoritarian leaders.',
       ['anti-communism mobilised elites and middle classes', 'national rebirth appealed to veterans', 'Maoism’s land reform message to peasants'],
       ['Nazi vote rose with the Depression', 'many voters were attracted by promises of order, not ideology', 'opportunism of leaders'],
       'Ideology shaped the movements, but economic crisis created the opportunity for them to gain mass support.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the ideologies of two authoritarian states, each from a different region.',
           ['Nazism: race, Lebensraum, Volksgemeinschaft', 'Fascism: state, corporatism', 'Maoism: peasants, class struggle', 'Castroism: nationalism, anti-imperialism, Marxism-Leninism after 1961'],
           'All rejected liberal democracy, but they differed fundamentally on class, race and property.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch08 Authoritarian Rule – Methods Used to Establish Power
# =============================================================================================
H('ch08-st1', 'the use of propaganda in the rise of Hitler and Mussolini',
  terms=[('Joseph Goebbels', 'the Nazi propaganda chief from 1930 and Reich Minister of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda from March 1933'),
         ('Il Popolo d’Italia', 'the newspaper founded by Mussolini in 1914, used to spread Fascist ideas'),
         ('mass rally', 'a large, carefully staged public meeting used to create a sense of unity and power, such as the Nuremberg rallies')],
  facts=[('In which year did Goebbels become head of Nazi propaganda?', '1930', ['1923', '1933', '1938'], 'Hitler appointed him Reich Propaganda Leader in 1930.'),
         ('Which press baron allied with the Nazis in the campaign against the Young Plan in 1929, giving them national publicity?', 'Alfred Hugenberg', ['Franz von Papen', 'Kurt von Schleicher', 'Gustav Stresemann'], 'Hugenberg’s DNVP and newspapers gave Hitler access to a national audience.'),
         ('What was innovative about Hitler’s 1932 election campaigns?', 'He flew between cities to speak at several rallies a day (“Hitler over Germany”)', ['He refused to give speeches', 'He used television broadcasts', 'He campaigned only in Bavaria'], 'Air travel showed him as modern and energetic.')],
  why=[('Explain why Nazi propaganda was effective in the years 1930–1932.', 'It targeted specific groups (farmers, workers, women) with tailored messages, used modern technology and mass rallies, and offered simple slogans of national unity and scapegoats during the Depression.'),
       ('Explain why propaganda alone does not explain the rise of Hitler or Mussolini.', 'Propaganda worked only where economic and political crisis made people receptive; elite intrigue and violence were also essential.'),
       ('Explain why Mussolini presented Fascism as a movement of action rather than doctrine.', 'Emphasising youth, energy and violence against socialists appealed to veterans and the middle class and allowed Fascism to adapt its programme opportunistically.')],
  contrast=[('Nazi propaganda before 1933', 'Nazi propaganda after 1933', 'Before 1933 it competed in elections to win votes; after 1933 it was a state monopoly controlling all media and aimed at mobilisation and consent.')],
  misc=[('Propaganda brainwashed the whole German population into supporting Hitler.', 'Propaganda reinforced existing beliefs more than it changed minds; the Nazis never won a majority in free elections and many Germans remained unconvinced.'),
        ('Mussolini’s propaganda was unimportant because he used only violence.', 'Mussolini was a former journalist who used Il Popolo d’Italia, rallies and myths of the March on Rome alongside squadrist violence.')],
  cases=[('the Nazi campaign for the July 1932 Reichstag election', 'The party used posters, films, gramophone records and Hitler’s flights; it became the largest party with 37.3%.',
          'the use of modern propaganda methods', 'It shows how organisation and technology turned economic discontent into votes.'),
         ('the myth of the March on Rome (October 1922)', 'Fascist propaganda presented the march as a heroic seizure of power, though Mussolini arrived by train after being invited to form a government.',
          'the use of propaganda to create legitimacy', 'The myth turned a political bargain into a revolutionary founding event, celebrated annually.')],
  src=[('The receptivity of the great masses is very limited, their intelligence is small, but their power of forgetting is enormous. In consequence of these facts, all effective propaganda must be limited to a very few points and must harp on these in slogans.',
        'Adolf Hitler, Mein Kampf, volume 1, 1925 (extract, translation)',
        'Propaganda must be simple and repetitive because the masses have limited understanding and memory.',
        'It reveals Hitler’s own ideas about propaganda before he came to power, which later guided Nazi methods.',
        'A self-promoting political book written in prison; it shows intentions, not the effectiveness of propaganda in practice.'),
       ('Nazi propaganda was most successful where it built on existing values – nationalism, anti-communism, fear of disorder – and least successful where it tried to change deeply held beliefs, such as religious loyalties.',
        'Paraphrased from David Welch, British historian, The Third Reich: Politics and Propaganda, 1993',
        'Propaganda succeeded mainly by reinforcing existing beliefs rather than converting people.',
        'Written by a specialist on propaganda, using a range of evidence including reports on public opinion.',
        'It is a general interpretation; it covers the whole Nazi period, not only the rise to power.')],
  cmp='Both discuss how propaganda influences people. Hitler believes the masses can be manipulated with simple repeated slogans, while Welch argues propaganda worked mainly by reinforcing existing beliefs; A is confident in manipulation, B shows its limits.',
  ev=[('Propaganda was the most important reason for Hitler’s rise to power.',
       ['Goebbels’ modern campaigns and tailored messages', 'Hitler myth and rallies', 'rise from 2.6% to 37.3%'],
       ['Depression created mass discontent', 'elite intrigue by Papen and Hindenburg', 'SA violence and weakness of opponents'],
       'Propaganda was vital in converting discontent into votes, but Hitler gained power through elite decisions in January 1933.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the use of propaganda by two authoritarian leaders in their rise to power.',
           ['Hitler: Goebbels, rallies, Hitler myth', 'Mussolini: Il Popolo d’Italia, myth of action', 'Mao: Yan’an and rural propaganda', 'common use of scapegoats and leader image'],
           'Both used propaganda to build mass followings, though its form reflected each society and the extent to which they also relied on force.')],
)

H('ch08-st2', 'the use of force and legal methods in the seizure of power in Germany and Italy',
  terms=[('Enabling Act', 'the law of 23 March 1933 that allowed Hitler’s cabinet to pass laws without the Reichstag for four years'),
         ('Reichstag Fire Decree', 'the decree of 28 February 1933 that suspended civil liberties after the Reichstag fire'),
         ('squadristi', 'Fascist armed squads that attacked socialists, trade unions and peasant leagues in 1920–1922'),
         ('Acerbo Law', 'the Italian electoral law of 1923 giving two-thirds of parliamentary seats to the party winning the most votes, provided it had at least 25%'),
         ('Night of the Long Knives', 'the purge of 30 June – 2 July 1934 in which Ernst Röhm and other SA leaders and opponents were murdered')],
  facts=[('When was the Reichstag building set on fire?', '27 February 1933', ['30 January 1933', '23 March 1933', '9 November 1923'], 'The Dutch communist Marinus van der Lubbe was arrested at the scene.'),
         ('By how many votes did the Reichstag pass the Enabling Act?', '444 to 94', ['288 to 196', '341 to 44', '500 to 0'], 'Only the SPD voted against; KPD deputies were arrested or in hiding.'),
         ('Which Italian socialist deputy was murdered by Fascists in June 1924?', 'Giacomo Matteotti', ['Antonio Gramsci', 'Filippo Turati', 'Luigi Sturzo'], 'His murder led to the Aventine Secession.'),
         ('Which king appointed Mussolini prime minister in October 1922?', 'Victor Emmanuel III', ['Umberto I', 'Victor Emmanuel II', 'Umberto II'], 'He refused to declare martial law against the March on Rome.')],
  why=[('Explain why the Reichstag Fire was useful to the Nazis.', 'It allowed Hitler to claim a communist uprising, persuade Hindenburg to sign the emergency decree suspending civil liberties, and arrest communist deputies before the March 1933 election.'),
       ('Explain why the Centre Party voted for the Enabling Act.', 'Hitler promised to respect the rights of the Catholic Church and the president’s powers, and Centre leaders feared violence and hoped for a concordat.'),
       ('Explain why Mussolini’s speech of 3 January 1925 was a turning point.', 'He accepted responsibility for the violence after the Matteotti crisis and moved to establish a dictatorship, banning opposition parties and the free press in 1925–1926.')],
  contrast=[('legal methods', 'the use of force', 'Legal methods worked through constitutional channels (appointment, decrees, the Enabling Act, the Acerbo Law); force used SA and squadristi violence and intimidation. Both Hitler and Mussolini combined them in a “legal revolution”.')],
  misc=[('Hitler seized power in a violent coup in January 1933.', 'Hitler was appointed chancellor legally by Hindenburg; his earlier coup attempt, the Munich Beer Hall Putsch of 1923, had failed.'),
        ('The Enabling Act was passed in a free and fair vote.', 'It was passed with SA and SS men surrounding the Kroll Opera House, communist deputies excluded and others intimidated.')],
  cases=[('the Munich Beer Hall Putsch of 8–9 November 1923', 'Hitler and Ludendorff tried to seize power in Bavaria; police fired on marchers and Hitler was imprisoned.',
          'the change from force to legal tactics', 'Its failure convinced Hitler to pursue power through elections and legality, while keeping the SA as a threat.'),
         ('the Matteotti crisis of 1924', 'After Matteotti denounced Fascist electoral fraud, he was murdered; opposition deputies left parliament (the Aventine Secession) but the king did not dismiss Mussolini.',
          'the combination of force and legality in Mussolini’s rise', 'Violence nearly toppled Mussolini, but the legal framework and royal support enabled him to consolidate his dictatorship.')],
  src=[('The government will make use of these powers only insofar as they are essential for carrying out vitally necessary measures. Neither the existence of the Reichstag nor that of the Reichsrat is threatened. The position and rights of the President remain unaffected.',
        'Adolf Hitler, speech to the Reichstag at the Kroll Opera House introducing the Enabling Act, 23 March 1933 (extract, translation)',
        'The Enabling Act will be used only when necessary and will not threaten the Reichstag, the states or the president.',
        'It shows the reassurances Hitler offered to win the two-thirds majority, revealing the legal strategy of the seizure of power.',
        'Its purpose was to persuade deputies, especially the Centre Party; the promises were broken within months, so it is evidence of tactics not intentions.'),
       ('Young SA and SS men lined the corridors and the square outside, chanting: “We want the Enabling Act – or there will be hell to pay!” We knew that our votes were being cast under threat.',
        'Paraphrased from the memoirs of Wilhelm Hoegner, Social Democratic Reichstag deputy present at the session of 23 March 1933, published in 1959',
        'The Enabling Act was passed in an atmosphere of intimidation by the SA.',
        'An eyewitness account from a deputy who opposed the Act.',
        'Written after 1945 by an opponent of the Nazis, it may be shaped by hindsight and a wish to explain the failure of resistance.')],
  cmp='Both describe the passing of the Enabling Act. Hitler presents it as a limited, legal measure respecting institutions, while the deputy describes it as passed under threat of violence; together they show the combination of legality and force.',
  ev=[('Hitler came to power legally.',
       ['appointed by Hindenburg on 30 January 1933', 'Enabling Act passed by more than two-thirds', 'use of Article 48 decrees'],
       ['SA violence and intimidation in the March 1933 election', 'KPD deputies arrested before the vote', 'Night of the Long Knives was extra-legal murder'],
       'Hitler’s seizure of power was a pseudo-legal revolution: legal forms disguised the systematic use of force.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did Hitler and Mussolini rely on the use of force to establish their power?',
           ['squadristi and SA violence', 'March on Rome and Reichstag Fire', 'Acerbo Law and Enabling Act', 'elite support: king and Hindenburg'],
           'Both relied on the threat of force, but legal methods and elite collaboration were decisive in taking office.')],
)

H('ch08-st3', 'the rise to power of Mao and the Chinese Communist Party',
  terms=[('Long March', 'the retreat of the Red Army from the Jiangxi Soviet in October 1934 to Shaanxi in October 1935, about 9,000–10,000 km'),
         ('Zunyi Conference', 'the CCP meeting of January 1935 at which Mao’s leadership of military strategy was recognised'),
         ('United Front', 'an alliance between the CCP and the Guomindang, first in 1923–1927 and again from 1937 against Japan'),
         ('guerrilla warfare', 'irregular fighting by small mobile units using local support, central to Mao’s military strategy')],
  facts=[('In which year was the Chinese Communist Party founded?', '1921', ['1911', '1927', '1949'], 'The First Congress met in Shanghai in July 1921.'),
         ('What happened in Shanghai in April 1927?', 'Jiang Jieshi’s forces massacred communists, ending the First United Front', ['Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic', 'The Long March began', 'Japan invaded Manchuria'], 'The White Terror destroyed the CCP’s urban base.'),
         ('Roughly what proportion of those who began the Long March from Jiangxi reached Shaanxi?', 'Less than one in ten', ['About half', 'About three-quarters', 'All of them'], 'About 86,000 set out and only some 8,000 arrived.'),
         ('On what date did Mao proclaim the People’s Republic of China?', '1 October 1949', ['1 January 1959', '4 May 1919', '7 July 1937'], 'He proclaimed it at Tiananmen Square in Beijing.'),
         ('What was the Xi’an Incident of December 1936?', 'The kidnapping of Jiang Jieshi by his own generals to force a united front against Japan', ['The CCP’s defeat at Xi’an', 'A Japanese bombing raid', 'Mao’s election as party chairman'], 'Zhang Xueliang detained Jiang until he agreed to cooperate with the CCP.')],
  why=[('Explain why the Long March became important to the CCP despite its heavy losses.', 'It preserved a core of leaders, confirmed Mao’s leadership at Zunyi, spread the party’s message along the route and became a heroic legend of endurance used in propaganda.'),
       ('Explain why the CCP won the Civil War of 1946–1949.', 'It had peasant support through land reform, disciplined forces and effective strategy (e.g. the Huaihai campaign), while the GMD suffered from corruption, hyperinflation, poor morale and military errors.'),
       ('Explain why the war against Japan strengthened the CCP.', 'It expanded base areas behind Japanese lines, recruited a large army and gained a patriotic reputation, while the GMD was weakened by heavy fighting.')],
  contrast=[('Mao’s rise to power', 'Hitler’s rise to power', 'Mao came to power through a long revolutionary civil war and peasant mobilisation; Hitler came to power through elections, propaganda and appointment within a constitutional framework.')],
  misc=[('The Long March was a victorious advance by the Red Army.', 'It was a retreat after defeat in Jiangxi during Jiang’s Fifth Encirclement Campaign, with enormous losses; only later was it mythologised.'),
        ('The CCP won mainly because of Soviet military aid.', 'Soviet aid (e.g. Japanese weapons captured in Manchuria in 1945) helped, but peasant support, land reform and GMD weakness were more important.')],
  cases=[('the Huaihai campaign (November 1948 – January 1949)', 'Communist forces destroyed about 500,000 Nationalist troops in east-central China.',
          'the military reasons for the CCP victory', 'It shows the CCP’s ability to fight large conventional battles and the collapse of GMD morale, opening the way to the Yangzi.'),
         ('the Yan’an Rectification Movement (1942–1944)', 'Party members were forced to study Mao’s writings and confess errors; opponents were purged.',
          'how Mao established his authority over the CCP', 'It consolidated Mao’s ideological supremacy and created methods later used in the PRC.')],
  src=[('The Long March is a manifesto, a propaganda force, a seeding machine. It has proclaimed to the world that the Red Army is an army of heroes. It has spread the seeds of revolution in eleven provinces.',
        'Mao Zedong, speech to party activists, December 1935 (adapted translation)',
        'The Long March was a heroic propaganda victory that spread the revolution across China.',
        'It shows how Mao immediately presented the Long March and built its myth.',
        'Mao’s purpose was to turn a costly retreat into a victory and to strengthen his own leadership; it omits the heavy losses.'),
       ('The Nationalists were not simply defeated by the Communists; they defeated themselves through corruption, inflation and the alienation of their own supporters. The Communists offered peasants land and a disciplined army.',
        'Paraphrased from Jonathan Fenby, British historian and journalist, Modern China: The Fall and Rise of a Great Power, 2008',
        'The GMD’s own failings – corruption, inflation and loss of support – were as important to its defeat as communist strength.',
        'A well-researched general history written with long hindsight.',
        'It is a broad survey, so it may simplify debates; a journalist-historian writing for a general audience.')],
  cmp='Both concern the reasons for communist success. Mao attributes success to the heroism of the Red Army and its spreading of revolution, while Fenby emphasises the GMD’s self-inflicted failures as well as CCP land policy; Mao stresses CCP strengths, Fenby GMD weaknesses.',
  ev=[('The weaknesses of the Guomindang were more important than the strengths of the CCP in explaining Mao’s victory in 1949.',
       ['corruption and hyperinflation', 'loss of urban middle-class support', 'military blunders in Manchuria'],
       ['land reform won peasant support', 'Mao’s guerrilla and mobile strategy', 'discipline and propaganda'],
       'Both mattered, but the CCP’s ability to exploit GMD weakness through peasant mobilisation was decisive.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the reasons for the communist victory in the Chinese Civil War (1946–1949).',
           ['GMD weaknesses', 'CCP land reform and peasant support', 'impact of the Sino-Japanese war', 'military strategy and leadership'],
           'The communist victory resulted from GMD weakness combined with the CCP’s successful mobilisation of peasants.')],
)

H('ch08-st4', 'charismatic leadership and the leader cult',
  terms=[('charismatic authority', 'Max Weber’s term for authority based on followers’ belief in the extraordinary personal qualities of a leader'),
         ('leader cult', 'the systematic glorification of a leader as infallible and heroic through propaganda, images and ritual'),
         ('Führerprinzip', 'the Nazi “leader principle” that the leader’s word is above all written law and authority flows downward'),
         ('“Hitler myth”', 'Ian Kershaw’s term for the popular image of Hitler as a heroic leader standing above the party, which bound Germans to the regime')],
  facts=[('Who wrote “The ‘Hitler Myth’: Image and Reality in the Third Reich” (1987)?', 'Ian Kershaw', ['A. J. P. Taylor', 'Hans Mommsen', 'Max Weber'], 'Kershaw studied how the image of Hitler created popular consent.'),
         ('Which publication, compiled under Lin Biao in 1964, became a central object of the Mao cult?', 'Quotations from Chairman Mao (the “Little Red Book”)', ['The Communist Manifesto', 'Mein Kampf', 'The Doctrine of Fascism'], 'It was distributed to the PLA and then to the population in hundreds of millions of copies.'),
         ('Which Fascist slogan expressed the cult of Mussolini?', '“Mussolini is always right”', ['“Ein Volk, ein Reich, ein Führer”', '“Serve the people”', '“Patria o muerte”'], '“Mussolini ha sempre ragione” was painted on walls across Italy.')],
  why=[('Explain why the leader cult was useful to authoritarian regimes.', 'It created an emotional bond with the population, provided legitimacy beyond the party, allowed the leader to stand above unpopular policies and officials and inspired obedience.'),
       ('Explain why Mao’s swim in the Yangzi in July 1966 was politically significant.', 'At 72 he publicly displayed vigour before launching the Cultural Revolution, reinforcing his cult and signalling he was back in control.'),
       ('Explain why Kershaw argued that the “Hitler myth” was essential to the Nazi regime.', 'Popular faith in Hitler as above party squabbles sustained consent even when the party and its policies were unpopular, until military defeat destroyed it after 1943.')],
  contrast=[('charismatic authority', 'legal-rational authority', 'Charismatic authority depends on belief in the leader’s extraordinary qualities; legal-rational authority depends on impersonal rules and offices, as in a constitutional state.')],
  misc=[('The leader cults were created spontaneously by popular enthusiasm.', 'They were deliberately constructed through propaganda by ministries and party organisations, though they built on genuine popular feeling.'),
        ('A leader cult means that the leader made every decision personally.', 'Leaders such as Hitler were often detached from administration; the cult masked a chaotic system of competing agencies.')],
  cases=[('the Nuremberg rallies, especially that filmed by Leni Riefenstahl in “Triumph of the Will” (1935)', 'Riefenstahl filmed the 1934 rally showing Hitler descending from the clouds by plane and reviewing massed ranks.',
          'the construction of the leader cult', 'The film presented Hitler as a messianic figure uniting the nation, central to the Hitler myth.'),
         ('the cult of Mao during the Cultural Revolution', 'Red Guards carried the Little Red Book, sang “The East is Red” and attended mass rallies in Tiananmen Square in 1966.',
          'how a leader cult can be used to mobilise against the party itself', 'Mao used his personal authority to bypass and attack the party leadership, showing the cult as an instrument of power.')],
  src=[('Hitler was popular not because of what the Nazi Party did but despite it. Germans saw him as a man above the corruption of the “little Hitlers”; when things went wrong, people said, “If only the Führer knew.”',
        'Paraphrased from Ian Kershaw, British historian, The ‘Hitler Myth’: Image and Reality in the Third Reich, 1987',
        'Hitler’s popularity rested on an image of him as above the party, so failures were blamed on subordinates.',
        'Based on extensive study of secret reports on public opinion (SOPADE, SD); a major scholarly work.',
        'It focuses on popular opinion rather than policy; the reports used may be biased by their authors’ purposes.'),
       ('[Poster] A smiling Mao in military uniform stands above a sea of workers, peasants and soldiers waving the Little Red Book; rays of light shine from behind his head. Caption: “Chairman Mao is the red sun in our hearts.”',
        'Chinese propaganda poster, 1967 (description)',
        'Mao is the source of light and life for the Chinese people, who are united in devotion to him.',
        'An official poster from the height of the Cultural Revolution, revealing the imagery of the Mao cult.',
        'It is propaganda showing the image the regime wished to project, not evidence of what people privately believed.')],
  cmp='Both show leaders presented as above ordinary politics. Kershaw analyses the Hitler myth as an image separating the leader from the party, while the poster itself creates a quasi-religious image of Mao as the sun of the people; A explains the cult, B is an example of it.',
  ev=[('The leader cult was the most important factor in maintaining support for authoritarian regimes.',
       ['Hitler myth sustained consent', 'Mao cult mobilised Red Guards', 'Mussolini ha sempre ragione'],
       ['terror and repression', 'economic recovery and benefits', 'nationalism and foreign policy success'],
       'The leader cult was vital to legitimacy, but it worked alongside terror and material benefits.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did charismatic leadership explain the rise to power of one authoritarian leader?',
           ['Hitler’s oratory and Führer image', 'Weber’s theory', 'economic crisis and elites', 'organisation and propaganda'],
           'Charisma was important, but it could only operate in the context of crisis and elite support.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch09 Authoritarian Rule – Consolidation and Maintenance of Power
# =============================================================================================
H('ch09-st1', 'the use of terror and repression in authoritarian states',
  terms=[('Gestapo', 'the Nazi secret state police, created in Prussia by Göring in 1933 and later controlled by Himmler and Heydrich'),
         ('SS', 'the Schutzstaffel, originally Hitler’s bodyguard, which under Himmler controlled the police and concentration camps'),
         ('Hundred Flowers Campaign', 'Mao’s invitation in 1956–1957 to intellectuals to criticise the party (“let a hundred flowers bloom”), followed by repression'),
         ('Anti-Rightist Campaign', 'the 1957–1959 purge in which over half a million people were labelled “rightists”, many sent to labour camps'),
         ('laogai', 'the Chinese system of forced labour camps, meaning “reform through labour”')],
  facts=[('Where was the first major Nazi concentration camp opened in March 1933?', 'Dachau', ['Auschwitz', 'Treblinka', 'Belsen'], 'Himmler announced Dachau near Munich on 20 March 1933.'),
         ('In which year did Mao launch the Hundred Flowers Campaign?', '1956', ['1949', '1966', '1976'], 'It began in 1956; criticism peaked in May 1957, followed by the Anti-Rightist Campaign.'),
         ('Approximately how many people were labelled “rightists” in the Anti-Rightist Campaign?', 'About 550,000', ['About 5,000', 'About 50 million', 'About 1,000'], 'The official figure was about 550,000.'),
         ('Which historian argued that the Gestapo depended heavily on denunciations by ordinary Germans?', 'Robert Gellately', ['A. J. P. Taylor', 'Lucy Dawidowicz', 'Hugh Trevor-Roper'], 'Gellately’s research on Würzburg showed that many investigations began with denunciations.'),
         ('What was the Italian Fascist secret police called?', 'OVRA', ['Okhrana', 'Stasi', 'Cheka'], 'OVRA was established in the late 1920s.')],
  why=[('Explain why historians argue that the Gestapo was not an all-seeing police force.', 'It had a small staff for a population of about 66 million (only a couple of dozen officials in some regional offices) and relied on denunciations by the public, so terror depended on popular participation.'),
       ('Explain why Mao launched the Anti-Rightist Campaign in 1957.', 'Criticism during the Hundred Flowers went far beyond what Mao expected, attacking the party’s monopoly; the campaign silenced critics and warned others.'),
       ('Explain why terror was combined with propaganda in authoritarian states.', 'Terror deterred opposition but could not create enthusiasm; propaganda sought genuine consent, so the two together maintained power.')],
  contrast=[('the use of terror in Nazi Germany', 'the use of terror in Fascist Italy', 'Nazi terror was large-scale, run by the SS and Gestapo with concentration camps and genocide; Fascist terror was more limited, relying on confino (internal exile), the OVRA and a special tribunal, with relatively few executions.')],
  misc=[('The Hundred Flowers Campaign was a genuine liberalisation that Mao intended to continue.', 'Historians disagree, but it was quickly reversed; whether it was a trap or a miscalculation, it led to the persecution of those who spoke out.'),
        ('Terror in Nazi Germany was directed equally at all Germans.', 'Terror targeted specific groups – communists, socialists, Jews, Roma, homosexuals, “asocials” – while most conformist Germans had little contact with the Gestapo.')],
  cases=[('the Night of the Long Knives (30 June – 2 July 1934)', 'The SS murdered Ernst Röhm, other SA leaders and opponents such as Kurt von Schleicher; about 85–200 were killed.',
          'the use of terror to consolidate power', 'It removed the SA as a rival to the army, won army support for Hitler as head of state, and showed that the regime would kill to protect itself.'),
         ('the Campaign to Suppress Counter-revolutionaries (1950–1951)', 'Mass trials and executions targeted former GMD members, landlords and “bandits”; Mao set killing quotas; estimates suggest 700,000 or more were executed.',
          'the use of terror to consolidate communist rule in China', 'It eliminated potential opposition and spread fear, establishing the party’s authority across society.')],
  src=[('Let a hundred flowers bloom, let a hundred schools of thought contend. Different forms and styles in art should develop freely, and different schools in science should contend freely.',
        'Mao Zedong, “On the Correct Handling of Contradictions Among the People”, speech to the Supreme State Conference, 27 February 1957 (extract, translation)',
        'Mao encourages open debate and free development in arts and science.',
        'An authoritative statement by Mao that launched the most intense phase of the campaign.',
        'The published version was revised in June 1957 after the Anti-Rightist Campaign began, adding limits on criticism, so it may not reflect the original speech.'),
       ('The Gestapo was not an omnipresent organisation. The office in Würzburg, responsible for the whole of Lower Franconia, had only a couple of dozen officials. Its success depended on the willingness of citizens to inform on their neighbours.',
        'Paraphrased from Robert Gellately, Canadian historian, The Gestapo and German Society, 1990',
        'The Gestapo was small and relied on denunciations from the public rather than its own surveillance.',
        'Based on surviving Gestapo case files, it provides detailed evidence of how terror worked locally.',
        'Würzburg may not represent all of Germany; files survive only for some areas and mainly show cases that were pursued.')],
  cmp='Both concern how regimes treated critics. Mao’s speech appears to invite free debate, while Gellately shows Nazi control relied on citizens denouncing neighbours; A suggests openness (later reversed), B shows the social basis of repression.',
  ev=[('Terror was the most important method used by authoritarian leaders to maintain power.',
       ['SS and camps in Germany', 'Anti-Rightist Campaign and laogai', 'Night of the Long Knives'],
       ['propaganda and the leader cult', 'economic benefits and recovery', 'popular consent and denunciation (Gellately)', 'Fascist Italy relied less on terror'],
       'Terror was essential, but regimes also relied heavily on consent built through propaganda and material benefits.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the use of terror by two authoritarian leaders, each from a different region.',
           ['Hitler: SS, Gestapo, camps', 'Mao: campaigns, Anti-Rightist, laogai', 'Mussolini: confino, OVRA', 'Castro: CDRs, UMAP camps'],
           'All used terror but its scale and targets differed; Nazi and Maoist terror was far greater than in Italy or Cuba.')],
)

H('ch09-st2', 'the control of education, the arts and mass media',
  terms=[('Reich Chamber of Culture', 'the body set up by Goebbels in September 1933 that every writer, artist, musician and journalist had to join'),
         ('Gleichschaltung', 'the Nazi process (literally “coordination”) of bringing all institutions, organisations and media under Nazi control'),
         ('Opera Nazionale Balilla', 'the Fascist youth organisation founded in 1926 to train Italian boys and girls in Fascist values'),
         ('socialist realism', 'the official artistic style in communist states, presenting idealised images of workers and leaders to promote socialism'),
         ('Degenerate Art exhibition', 'the Nazi exhibition in Munich in 1937 that ridiculed modern art as “un-German”')],
  facts=[('When did Nazi students burn books in Berlin and other university towns?', '10 May 1933', ['30 January 1933', '9 November 1938', '1 September 1939'], 'Works by Jewish, Marxist and liberal authors were burned.'),
         ('What was the “Volksempfänger”?', 'A cheap radio set promoted so that Germans could hear Nazi broadcasts', ['A Nazi newspaper', 'A youth organisation', 'A film studio'], 'By 1939 about 70% of households had a radio.'),
         ('In 1942 Mao defined the role of art in China in which speech?', 'The Yan’an Talks on Literature and Art', ['The Hundred Flowers speech', 'The Hunan Report', 'The May Fourth Declaration'], 'He declared that art must serve the workers, peasants and soldiers and the party.')],
  why=[('Explain why authoritarian regimes placed so much emphasis on education.', 'Schools could shape the next generation’s values and loyalty; curricula were rewritten (race biology in Germany, Fascist textbooks in Italy), teachers were purged or forced into party organisations.'),
       ('Explain why radio was so important to Nazi propaganda.', 'It allowed Hitler’s speeches to reach every home; cheap sets, loudspeakers in public places and control of all broadcasting made it the main channel of the regime’s message.'),
       ('Explain why some historians think control of culture in Fascist Italy was incomplete.', 'The Catholic Church kept its own education and youth groups after 1929, cultural elites retained some independence, and many Italians were indifferent to propaganda.')],
  contrast=[('control of the arts in Nazi Germany', 'control of the arts in Mao’s China', 'Both demanded political conformity, but Nazi culture promoted racial and völkisch themes through the Chamber of Culture, while Maoist culture promoted class struggle; under the Cultural Revolution only eight “model operas” were permitted.')],
  misc=[('All Germans eagerly listened to Nazi radio broadcasts.', 'Many found the propaganda dull; light entertainment was increased to keep audiences, and some listened illegally to the BBC during the war.'),
        ('Fascist Italy had total control over education.', 'Catholic schools and Azione Cattolica continued under the Lateran Pacts of 1929, limiting Fascist control.')],
  cases=[('the Nazi school curriculum after 1933', 'History, biology (race science) and physical education were emphasised; textbooks glorified the Nazi struggle; teachers had to join the National Socialist Teachers’ League.',
          'the use of education to indoctrinate youth', 'The curriculum aimed to produce racially conscious, obedient and physically fit citizens loyal to Hitler.'),
         ('the eight model operas of the Cultural Revolution', 'Jiang Qing promoted a handful of revolutionary operas and ballets such as “The Red Detachment of Women”; most other art was banned.',
          'the extent of cultural control in Maoist China', 'It shows near-total state control of culture and the political role of art.')],
  src=[('These boys and girls enter our organisations at ten years of age… They will not be free again for the rest of their lives.',
        'Adolf Hitler, speech at Reichenberg, 2 December 1938 (extract, translation)',
        'Nazi organisations will control young Germans from childhood and throughout their lives.',
        'Hitler’s own statement of the regime’s aim of total control over youth.',
        'It is a public speech expressing an ambition; it shows intentions, not how far the regime actually succeeded.'),
       ('The Hitler Youth never achieved the total control it claimed. By the late 1930s, compulsory membership bred boredom and resentment; groups such as the Edelweiss Pirates and the Swing Youth rejected its discipline.',
        'Paraphrased from Detlev Peukert, German historian, Inside Nazi Germany: Conformity, Opposition and Racism in Everyday Life, 1987',
        'Nazi youth control was incomplete; compulsion caused boredom and some young people formed nonconformist groups.',
        'Based on research into everyday life using police and youth records, giving a social history perspective.',
        'It focuses on nonconformity, which may make it seem more widespread than it was; it is a selective social history.')],
  cmp='Both concern Nazi control of young people. Hitler claims the regime will control youth totally and permanently, whereas Peukert argues that control was incomplete, producing boredom and nonconformity; A shows aims, B results.',
  ev=[('Authoritarian regimes were successful in controlling education and the arts.',
       ['Reich Chamber of Culture and Gleichschaltung', 'Hitler Youth compulsory from 1936/1939', 'model operas and Cultural Revolution'],
       ['Church schools in Italy', 'nonconformist youth groups', 'private indifference and humour', 'intellectual emigration'],
       'Regimes achieved extensive formal control, but private beliefs and cultural life were harder to control.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did authoritarian leaders succeed in controlling the minds of young people?',
           ['Hitler Youth and schools', 'Balilla in Italy', 'Red Guards in China', 'limits: Edelweiss Pirates, Church, indifference'],
           'They mobilised many young people but never achieved total control over beliefs.')],
)

H('ch09-st3', 'the treatment of opposition in authoritarian states',
  terms=[('Concordat (1933)', 'the agreement of July 1933 between Nazi Germany and the Vatican guaranteeing Catholic rights in return for Church withdrawal from politics'),
         ('Lateran Pacts', 'the agreements of 11 February 1929 between Mussolini and the Pope, recognising Vatican City and Catholicism as the state religion'),
         ('Confessing Church', 'the Protestant group formed in 1934 by pastors such as Martin Niemöller and Dietrich Bonhoeffer to resist Nazi control of the Church'),
         ('German Labour Front (DAF)', 'the Nazi organisation under Robert Ley that replaced trade unions after they were abolished on 2 May 1933')],
  facts=[('On what date were German trade unions abolished?', '2 May 1933', ['1 April 1933', '14 July 1933', '30 June 1934'], 'SA and SS occupied union offices the day after the May Day celebrations.'),
         ('Which Catholic bishop publicly condemned the Nazi “euthanasia” programme in 1941?', 'Clemens von Galen, Bishop of Münster', ['Martin Niemöller', 'Ludwig Müller', 'Dietrich Bonhoeffer'], 'His sermons of August 1941 contributed to the official halt of the T4 programme.'),
         ('Which Chinese marshal was purged after criticising the Great Leap Forward at the Lushan Conference in 1959?', 'Peng Dehuai', ['Lin Biao', 'Zhou Enlai', 'Deng Xiaoping'], 'Peng’s letter to Mao led to his dismissal as defence minister.'),
         ('By which law did the Nazi Party become the only legal party in Germany?', 'The Law against the Formation of New Parties, 14 July 1933', ['The Enabling Act', 'The Nuremberg Laws', 'The Reichstag Fire Decree'], 'All other parties had been banned or had dissolved themselves.')],
  why=[('Explain why Hitler signed a Concordat with the Catholic Church in 1933.', 'It reassured Catholics, gained international prestige, and removed the Church from politics (the Centre Party dissolved), while Hitler intended to break its terms later.'),
       ('Explain why Mussolini signed the Lateran Pacts.', 'Reconciliation with the Papacy ended the “Roman Question” of 1870, won Catholic support and legitimacy for the regime, at the cost of allowing Church autonomy.'),
       ('Explain why opposition to the Nazi regime was so limited.', 'Terror, the destruction of parties and unions, propaganda, economic recovery and the popularity of Hitler isolated opponents, while the left was divided and the elites largely collaborated.')],
  contrast=[('resistance', 'nonconformity', 'Resistance means active attempts to undermine or overthrow the regime (e.g. the July Plot of 1944); nonconformity means refusing to conform in limited ways, such as telling jokes or listening to banned music.')],
  misc=[('The Christian churches in Germany organised mass resistance to Nazism.', 'Individuals such as Niemöller, Bonhoeffer and von Galen protested, but the churches as institutions mostly accommodated the regime to protect their own position.'),
        ('Mao never faced opposition within the CCP after 1949.', 'Leaders such as Peng Dehuai (1959) and later Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping questioned his policies; Mao responded with purges and the Cultural Revolution.')],
  cases=[('the arrest of Martin Niemöller in 1937', 'The founder of the Pastors’ Emergency League was imprisoned and spent 1938–1945 in Sachsenhausen and Dachau as Hitler’s “personal prisoner”.',
          'the treatment of religious opposition in Nazi Germany', 'It shows that outspoken clergy faced imprisonment, though the regime avoided an open battle with the churches.'),
         ('the purge of Peng Dehuai at the Lushan Conference (July–August 1959)', 'Peng criticised the Great Leap Forward in a private letter; Mao circulated it and denounced him as leader of an “anti-party clique”.',
          'the treatment of opposition within the ruling party', 'It deterred honest reporting of the famine and showed that even senior leaders could not criticise Mao.')],
  src=[('First they came for the socialists, and I did not speak out – because I was not a socialist. Then they came for the trade unionists, and I did not speak out – because I was not a trade unionist. Then they came for me – and there was no one left to speak for me.',
        'Martin Niemöller, German Protestant pastor, words from his post-war lectures, c. 1946 (widely quoted version)',
        'Germans, including Niemöller, failed to defend others as the Nazis destroyed each group of opponents, until no one was left to resist.',
        'A reflection by a former prisoner and Church leader on the failure of opposition, showing how the regime isolated groups one by one.',
        'It was spoken after the war, with hindsight and moral purpose; several versions exist, and Niemöller had initially welcomed Hitler in 1933.'),
       ('The trade unions are dead. In their place stands the German Labour Front, which embraces employer and worker alike. The class struggle is over.',
        'Paraphrased from Robert Ley, leader of the German Labour Front, speech of May 1933',
        'Trade unions have been destroyed and replaced by an organisation that unites workers and employers, ending class conflict.',
        'It shows the Nazi justification for destroying the unions, from the official responsible.',
        'It is propaganda that disguises the loss of workers’ rights to strike and bargain.')],
  cmp='Both refer to the destruction of the trade unions. Niemöller sees it as one stage in the elimination of all opposition and laments the failure to resist, while Ley celebrates it as the end of class struggle; A is a critical reflection, B regime propaganda.',
  ev=[('Authoritarian regimes dealt with opposition mainly through repression.',
       ['banning parties and unions', 'imprisonment of Niemöller', 'purge of Peng Dehuai'],
       ['Concordat and Lateran Pacts as compromise', 'DAF benefits like Strength through Joy', 'propaganda and consent'],
       'Repression was central, but regimes also used compromise and incentives to neutralise opponents.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the methods used by authoritarian leaders to deal with opposition.',
           ['repression of political parties', 'compromise with churches', 'purges within the party', 'success and limits of each'],
           'Leaders combined repression with compromise, with repression decisive against political opponents and compromise more common with churches.')],
)

H('ch09-st4', 'the use of foreign policy to maintain power',
  terms=[('remilitarisation of the Rhineland', 'Hitler’s move of German troops into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936, breaking the Treaty of Versailles and Locarno'),
         ('Anschluss', 'the union of Germany and Austria in March 1938, approved by a plebiscite with over 99% support'),
         ('Resist America, Aid Korea', 'the mass campaign accompanying China’s intervention in the Korean War (1950–1953), used to mobilise the population')],
  facts=[('In which year did Italy invade Abyssinia?', '1935', ['1923', '1938', '1940'], 'The conquest was completed in May 1936 and celebrated as a Fascist triumph.'),
         ('Chinese troops entered the Korean War in which year?', '1950', ['1949', '1953', '1945'], 'Chinese “People’s Volunteers” crossed the Yalu in October 1950.')],
  why=[('Explain why foreign policy successes increased support for Hitler.', 'Breaking Versailles without war – the Saar (1935), the Rhineland (1936), the Anschluss (1938) – restored national pride and strengthened the Hitler myth.'),
       ('Explain why the Korean War helped Mao consolidate power.', 'The war rallied patriotism against the USA, justified campaigns against “counter-revolutionaries” and enhanced China’s international prestige.')],
  contrast=[('foreign policy as a tool of domestic consolidation', 'foreign policy driven by ideology', 'Foreign policy can be used to win popularity and unity at home; ideologically driven policy pursues aims such as Lebensraum regardless of domestic effects.')],
  misc=[('Foreign policy always strengthened authoritarian regimes.', 'Failures could weaken them: Mussolini’s military defeats in 1940–1943 led to his dismissal in July 1943.')],
  cases=[('the Abyssinian War (1935–1936)', 'Italy used poison gas and conquered Ethiopia; Mussolini proclaimed an empire in May 1936.',
          'the use of foreign policy to strengthen the regime', 'Victory over League sanctions produced a peak of popularity for Mussolini, though it drew him closer to Hitler.'),
         ('the Bay of Pigs invasion (April 1961)', 'Castro’s forces defeated about 1,400 CIA-trained Cuban exiles within three days.',
          'the use of external threat to consolidate power', 'Victory boosted Castro’s prestige, allowed mass arrests of suspected opponents and led him to declare the revolution socialist.')],
  src=[('[Photograph] Cheering crowds in Cologne greet German soldiers marching across the Hohenzollern bridge; women throw flowers at the troops.',
        'Photograph of the remilitarisation of the Rhineland, Cologne, 7 March 1936, published in the German press (description)',
        'Germans in the Rhineland enthusiastically welcomed the return of German troops.',
        'It shows popular enthusiasm for one of Hitler’s foreign policy coups.',
        'Published in a controlled press, it may be selected or staged; it does not show those who were indifferent or worried about war.'),
       ('Hitler’s foreign policy successes before 1939 were his greatest source of popularity. Each bloodless victory reinforced the belief that the Führer could restore Germany’s greatness without war.',
        'Paraphrased from Ian Kershaw, British historian, Hitler 1936–1945: Nemesis, 2000',
        'Hitler’s popularity rested heavily on bloodless foreign successes that restored national greatness.',
        'An authoritative biography based on wide research, including public-opinion reports.',
        'It is an interpretation; it focuses on Hitler and may give less attention to economic sources of support.')],
  cmp='Both show popular approval of Hitler’s foreign policy. The photograph shows a specific moment of enthusiasm in 1936, while Kershaw argues more generally that bloodless victories were his greatest source of popularity; A is evidence, B interpretation.',
  ev=[('Foreign policy was the most effective method used by authoritarian leaders to maintain popular support.',
       ['Rhineland and Anschluss for Hitler', 'Abyssinia for Mussolini', 'Korea for Mao', 'Bay of Pigs for Castro'],
       ['economic recovery and employment', 'terror and propaganda', 'failures later weakened regimes (Mussolini 1943)'],
       'Foreign successes produced peaks of support, but they were short-lived and regimes relied more consistently on domestic methods.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was foreign policy used by two authoritarian leaders to maintain power?',
           ['Hitler 1935–1938', 'Mussolini 1935–1936', 'Mao and Korea', 'Castro and the Cold War'],
           'Foreign policy was a significant tool, but its effect depended on success and was secondary to domestic control.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch10 Authoritarian Rule – Domestic Policies and Their Impact
# =============================================================================================
H('ch10-st1', 'the economic policies of authoritarian states',
  terms=[('Four-Year Plan', 'the Nazi economic programme launched in 1936 under Hermann Göring to make Germany ready for war and self-sufficient within four years'),
         ('Great Leap Forward', 'Mao’s campaign of 1958–1962 to transform China rapidly through people’s communes and mass industrial projects, which caused a catastrophic famine'),
         ('people’s commune', 'a huge collective unit created in 1958, combining villages for agriculture, industry, administration and communal living'),
         ('Battle for Grain', 'Mussolini’s campaign from 1925 to make Italy self-sufficient in wheat'),
         ('Mefo bills', 'credit notes devised by Hjalmar Schacht to finance secret rearmament off the official budget')],
  facts=[('Who was placed in charge of the Four-Year Plan in 1936?', 'Hermann Göring', ['Hjalmar Schacht', 'Albert Speer', 'Robert Ley'], 'Göring’s appointment marked the shift from Schacht’s recovery policies to war preparation.'),
         ('In which year did Mao launch the Great Leap Forward?', '1958', ['1949', '1953', '1966'], 'It followed the First Five-Year Plan (1953–1957).'),
         ('Which of the following was a feature of the Great Leap Forward?', 'Backyard steel furnaces', ['Private farming encouraged by the state', 'Foreign investment zones', 'The New Economic Policy'], 'Millions melted pots and tools to meet steel targets, producing largely useless pig iron.'),
         ('What was “Quota 90” in Fascist Italy?', 'The revaluation of the lira to 90 to the British pound in 1927', ['A target of 90 million tonnes of grain', 'A 90% tax on imports', 'A plan to raise the population to 90 million'], 'The “Battle for the Lira” boosted prestige but harmed exports.'),
         ('What were Cuba’s “ten million tons” in 1970?', 'The sugar harvest target, which was not achieved', ['The tonnage of Soviet oil imports', 'The output of nickel', 'The tobacco export target'], 'About 8.5 million tons were harvested; the failure damaged other sectors.')],
  why=[('Explain why unemployment fell rapidly in Nazi Germany between 1933 and 1938.', 'Public works such as the autobahns, the Reich Labour Service, conscription from 1935, rearmament and the removal of women and Jews from the statistics reduced registered unemployment from about 6 million to under 500,000.'),
       ('Explain why the Great Leap Forward led to famine.', 'Unrealistic targets, false reports of record harvests, high state grain procurement, labour diverted to steel production, communal mess halls and the purge of critics after Lushan combined to cause mass starvation.'),
       ('Explain why autarky was central to Nazi economic policy.', 'Self-sufficiency would protect Germany from blockade, as in 1914–1918, and was needed for a war of conquest for Lebensraum.')],
  contrast=[('the Four-Year Plan', 'the Great Leap Forward', 'The Four-Year Plan prepared a capitalist industrial economy for war through state direction and synthetic substitutes; the Great Leap tried to transform a peasant economy through mass mobilisation and communes, with catastrophic results.'),
            ('Schacht’s New Plan (1934)', 'Göring’s Four-Year Plan (1936)', 'Schacht controlled imports through bilateral trade agreements while balancing recovery and rearmament; Göring prioritised rearmament and autarky regardless of cost.')],
  misc=[('The Nazis achieved full economic self-sufficiency by 1939.', 'Autarky failed: in 1939 Germany still imported about a third of its raw materials, and synthetic oil and rubber targets were not met.'),
        ('The famine during the Great Leap Forward was caused mainly by bad weather.', 'Weather played a part, but historians stress policy: excessive procurement, false reporting and the refusal to change course after 1959.')],
  cases=[('the Lushan Conference of July–August 1959', 'Peng Dehuai criticised the Great Leap; Mao purged him and intensified the campaign.',
          'the political causes of economic disaster', 'Fear of punishment led officials to exaggerate output, deepening the famine of 1959–1961.'),
         ('the Battle for Grain (from 1925)', 'Wheat output rose substantially, but land was diverted from more suitable crops such as olives and fruit, and imports of other foods increased.',
          'the limits of Fascist economic policy', 'Propaganda success came at an economic cost, showing that prestige often outweighed efficiency.')],
  src=[('I thus set the following task: I. The German armed forces must be operational within four years. II. The German economy must be fit for war within four years.',
        'Adolf Hitler, secret memorandum on the Four-Year Plan, August 1936 (extract, translation)',
        'The economy and the armed forces must be prepared for war within four years.',
        'A secret document by Hitler himself, revealing the true aims of economic policy without propaganda.',
        'It shows intentions, not results; as a single memorandum it does not show how the plan was implemented or the debates with Schacht.'),
       ('In the Great Leap Forward there has been a certain petty-bourgeois fanaticism. We wanted to enter communism in one step. The mass smelting of steel wasted resources and manpower.',
        'Paraphrased from Peng Dehuai, Minister of Defence, letter to Mao Zedong at the Lushan Conference, July 1959',
        'The Great Leap Forward was driven by over-enthusiasm and unrealistic ambitions, and the steel campaign wasted resources.',
        'A senior leader’s first-hand criticism of the policy while it was under way.',
        'It was written in a cautious, respectful tone to avoid offending Mao, so it understates the scale of the disaster.')],
  cmp='Both concern ambitious state economic plans. Hitler sets clear, urgent targets to prepare for war, while Peng criticises the over-ambitious pace and waste of the Great Leap; A sets aims, B shows the cost of pursuing such aims.',
  ev=[('The economic policies of authoritarian regimes were driven more by ideology than by economic logic.',
       ['Lebensraum and war preparation in the Four-Year Plan', 'Great Leap’s utopian communism', 'Quota 90 for prestige'],
       ['Nazi recovery used pragmatic methods (Schacht)', 'First Five-Year Plan achieved growth', 'Cuba adapted to Soviet trade'],
       'Ideology set the goals, and when it overrode economic logic the results were costly, most disastrously in China.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the success of the economic policies of two authoritarian states, each from a different region.',
           ['Germany: unemployment and rearmament vs autarky failure', 'China: First Five-Year Plan vs Great Leap famine', 'Italy: Battle for Grain and corporate state', 'Cuba: sugar dependence'],
           'Success depended on aims: Nazi policy met its war-preparation aims more than Maoist policy met its development aims.')],
)

H('ch10-st2', 'the social policies of authoritarian states towards women, youth and the family',
  terms=[('Kinder, Küche, Kirche', 'the slogan (“children, kitchen, church”) summarising the Nazi ideal role for women'),
         ('Mother’s Cross', 'the Nazi award from 1938 for mothers of four (bronze), six (silver) or eight or more (gold) children'),
         ('Marriage Law (1950)', 'the Chinese communist law that banned arranged marriages, concubinage and child marriage and allowed divorce'),
         ('Hitler Youth', 'the Nazi youth organisation, compulsory for boys from 1936 (strengthened in 1939); girls joined the League of German Girls (BDM)')],
  facts=[('What did Nazi marriage loans (from 1933) encourage?', 'Women to leave work and have children, with a quarter of the loan cancelled for each child', ['Women to join the army', 'Couples to emigrate', 'Women to become teachers'], 'Loans were available if the wife gave up paid work.'),
         ('Which Fascist campaign from 1927 aimed to increase the Italian population?', 'The Battle for Births', ['The Battle for Grain', 'The Battle for the Lira', 'The Battle for Land'], 'Mussolini aimed at 60 million Italians; the birth rate nevertheless fell.'),
         ('Which Chinese communist law of 1950 transformed the legal position of women?', 'The Marriage Law', ['The Agrarian Reform Law', 'The Constitution of 1982', 'The One-Child Policy'], 'It allowed free choice of partner and divorce.')],
  why=[('Explain why the Nazis wanted women to stay at home.', 'Nazi ideology saw women’s role as producing racially pure children for the Volksgemeinschaft; removing women from work also reduced male unemployment.'),
       ('Explain why Nazi policy towards women changed after 1937.', 'Rearmament created labour shortages, so women were increasingly needed in industry, and the ban on married women in the civil service was relaxed.'),
       ('Explain why the Chinese Communist Party promoted women’s equality.', 'Marxist ideology linked women’s liberation with class liberation, and women’s labour was needed for collectivisation and industrialisation.')],
  contrast=[('Nazi policy towards women', 'Maoist policy towards women', 'Nazi policy emphasised motherhood and the home; Maoist policy proclaimed equality and mobilised women into the workforce (“women hold up half the sky”), though traditional burdens persisted.')],
  misc=[('Nazi policies succeeded in removing women from the workforce.', 'Women’s employment actually rose in the later 1930s as rearmament created labour shortages; the birth rate rose only modestly.'),
        ('The Battle for Births greatly increased Italy’s birth rate.', 'Despite bachelor taxes and incentives, the birth rate continued to fall during the 1930s.')],
  cases=[('the Lebensborn programme (from 1935)', 'The SS set up homes where “racially valuable” unmarried mothers could give birth, and later kidnapped children from occupied territories.',
          'the racial basis of Nazi family policy', 'It shows that Nazi family policy was driven by racial goals rather than traditional morality.'),
         ('communal mess halls during the Great Leap Forward', 'Families ate in commune canteens and private cooking was banned, freeing women for field and furnace work.',
          'the impact of communist policy on the family', 'The family was subordinated to the collective, but when food ran out the canteens became centres of famine.')],
  src=[('If the man’s world is said to be the State, his struggle, his readiness to devote himself to the service of the community, then it may perhaps be said that the woman’s is a smaller world. For her world is her husband, her family, her children and her home.',
        'Adolf Hitler, speech to the National Socialist Women’s League, Nuremberg, 8 September 1934 (extract, translation)',
        'Men belong in public life and the state, while women’s world is the home and family.',
        'Hitler’s own statement of Nazi ideology on women, delivered to the party’s women’s organisation.',
        'It is an ideological speech, so it states ideals rather than actual policy, which changed when women’s labour was needed.'),
       ('Nazi policies towards women were full of contradictions. The regime wanted women at home, yet by 1939 more women were in paid work than in 1933, and the birth rate, although higher, never reached the levels propaganda demanded.',
        'Paraphrased from Jill Stephenson, British historian, Women in Nazi Germany, 2001',
        'Nazi policy on women was contradictory and only partly successful: women’s employment rose and birth-rate gains were limited.',
        'A specialist scholarly study using statistical and archival evidence.',
        'It is a synthesis focused on outcomes; it may say less about the experiences of individual women.')],
  cmp='Both concern the role of women in Nazi Germany. Hitler insists that women’s world is the home, while Stephenson shows that in practice more women were working by 1939 and policies were contradictory; A gives ideals, B outcomes.',
  ev=[('The social policies of authoritarian states achieved their aims.',
       ['Hitler Youth membership near universal by 1939', 'Marriage Law changed women’s legal status', 'birth rate rose in Germany after 1933'],
       ['women’s employment rose in Germany', 'Italian birth rate fell', 'traditional attitudes persisted in rural China', 'youth nonconformity'],
       'Social policies changed institutions and behaviour in part, but rarely achieved the transformation the regimes sought.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the impact of the policies of two authoritarian states on women.',
           ['Nazi Germany: motherhood, marriage loans, later mobilisation', 'Mao’s China: Marriage Law, communes', 'Fascist Italy: Battle for Births', 'outcomes and limits'],
           'Nazi and Fascist policy aimed to return women to the home while Maoist policy proclaimed equality; in all cases ideology clashed with economic need.')],
)

H('ch10-st3', 'the treatment of minorities in authoritarian states',
  terms=[('Nuremberg Laws', 'the laws of 15 September 1935 that stripped Jews of German citizenship and banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and non-Jewish Germans'),
         ('Kristallnacht', 'the pogrom of 9–10 November 1938 in which synagogues, Jewish homes and businesses were attacked and about 30,000 Jewish men were sent to camps'),
         ('Holocaust', 'the systematic, state-sponsored murder of about six million European Jews by Nazi Germany and its collaborators, 1941–1945'),
         ('Wannsee Conference', 'the meeting of senior Nazi officials on 20 January 1942, chaired by Reinhard Heydrich, to coordinate the “Final Solution”')],
  facts=[('When did the Nazi boycott of Jewish shops take place?', '1 April 1933', ['15 September 1935', '9 November 1938', '20 January 1942'], 'The one-day boycott was the first nationally organised anti-Jewish action.'),
         ('In which year did the People’s Liberation Army enter Tibet?', '1950', ['1949', '1959', '1966'], 'After the Seventeen Point Agreement (1951), Tibet was incorporated into the PRC; the 1959 uprising led the Dalai Lama to flee to India.'),
         ('In which year did Fascist Italy introduce anti-Jewish racial laws?', '1938', ['1922', '1929', '1943'], 'The laws excluded Jews from schools, professions and the armed forces.'),
         ('Who chaired the Wannsee Conference?', 'Reinhard Heydrich', ['Adolf Eichmann', 'Heinrich Himmler', 'Hermann Göring'], 'Eichmann took the minutes; Heydrich chaired.')],
  why=[('Explain why the Nazi persecution of Jews became more radical between 1933 and 1941.', 'Ideological antisemitism, competition between agencies, the absence of opposition, the conquest of Poland and the USSR with millions of Jews, and the war of annihilation pushed policy from exclusion to emigration to mass murder.'),
       ('Explain why the Nuremberg Laws were significant.', 'They legally defined who was a Jew by ancestry, removed citizenship and forbade intermarriage, turning racial ideology into law and isolating Jews from German society.'),
       ('Explain why the Chinese government treated Tibet as a threat in 1959.', 'The Lhasa uprising challenged communist authority and Chinese sovereignty; Beijing saw Tibetan Buddhism and the Dalai Lama as rival sources of loyalty.')],
  contrast=[('persecution of Jews in Nazi Germany', 'treatment of ethnic minorities in Mao’s China', 'Nazi policy was based on biological racism and culminated in genocide; Maoist policy aimed at class struggle and assimilation, suppressing religion and culture, particularly during the Cultural Revolution, rather than racial extermination.')],
  misc=[('The Holocaust was planned in detail from the moment Hitler came to power in 1933.', 'Historians disagree, but most see policy radicalising over time; systematic mass murder developed in 1941–1942 during the war in the East.'),
        ('Fascist Italy persecuted Jews in the same way as Nazi Germany from 1922.', 'Italy had no state antisemitism until 1938, and deportations to death camps began only after the German occupation of 1943.')],
  cases=[('Kristallnacht (9–10 November 1938)', 'After the assassination of a German diplomat in Paris by Herschel Grynszpan, Goebbels orchestrated attacks; over 90 Jews were killed and Jews were fined one billion marks.',
          'the escalation of anti-Jewish policy', 'It marked a shift from legal discrimination to open violence and forced emigration, and the elimination of Jews from economic life.'),
         ('the Tibetan uprising of March 1959', 'Fearing the arrest of the Dalai Lama, Tibetans rose in Lhasa; the PLA crushed the revolt and the Dalai Lama fled to India.',
          'the treatment of ethnic minorities in China', 'It led to harsh repression, collectivisation and the destruction of monasteries, showing the subordination of minority autonomy to party control.')],
  src=[('Marriages between Jews and citizens of German or kindred blood are forbidden. Marriages concluded in defiance of this law are void. Extramarital sexual intercourse between Jews and citizens of German or kindred blood is forbidden.',
        'Law for the Protection of German Blood and German Honour (one of the Nuremberg Laws), 15 September 1935 (extract, translation)',
        'Jews are forbidden to marry or have sexual relations with Germans, and such marriages are void.',
        'An official legal text, it shows exactly how racial ideology was turned into law.',
        'A legal text does not reveal how it was enforced, how Germans reacted or how Jews experienced it.'),
       ('In many areas of Tibet the monasteries have been emptied, religious practice is banned and many people died of hunger. If this continues, the Tibetan nationality is in danger of extinction.',
        'Paraphrased from the Panchen Lama, “70,000 Character Petition” submitted to Zhou Enlai and Mao Zedong, 1962',
        'Chinese policies had destroyed religion, caused famine and threatened the survival of the Tibetan people.',
        'Written by a senior Tibetan religious leader who worked within the Chinese system, it provides insider evidence.',
        'It was written to persuade the leadership to change policy, so it emphasises suffering; it led to the Panchen Lama’s imprisonment and was suppressed for decades.')],
  cmp='Both describe policies that threatened minorities. The Nuremberg Laws are an official legal measure imposing racial segregation, while the Panchen Lama’s petition is a protest from within describing the destructive effects of policy; A is from the persecutor, B from a victim’s representative.',
  ev=[('Ideology was the main reason for the persecution of minorities in authoritarian states.',
       ['Nazi racial antisemitism', 'class struggle and atheism in Maoism', 'Italian race laws after alliance with Germany'],
       ['war and territorial expansion radicalised policy', 'scapegoating for political gain', 'opportunism and competition between agencies'],
       'Ideology defined the targets, but war and the dynamics of the regimes determined the scale and timing of persecution.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did the treatment of minorities in Nazi Germany change between 1933 and 1945?',
           ['legal discrimination 1933–1935', 'Nuremberg Laws', 'Kristallnacht 1938', 'ghettos and Einsatzgruppen', 'Wannsee and the death camps'],
           'Policy changed from discrimination to genocide, though its antisemitic goals were continuous.')],
)

H('ch10-st4', 'the Cultural Revolution and the limits of totalitarian control',
  terms=[('Cultural Revolution', 'the mass political campaign launched by Mao in 1966 to purge “capitalist roaders” and revive revolutionary zeal, lasting until his death in 1976'),
         ('Red Guards', 'radical students mobilised by Mao in 1966 to attack “bourgeois” officials, teachers and the “Four Olds”'),
         ('Four Olds', 'old customs, old culture, old habits and old ideas, targeted for destruction by Red Guards in 1966'),
         ('Gang of Four', 'the radical leaders Jiang Qing, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen, arrested in October 1976'),
         ('Down to the Countryside Movement', 'the policy from 1968 of sending about 17 million urban youths to rural areas')],
  facts=[('In which year did Mao launch the Cultural Revolution?', '1966', ['1958', '1949', '1976'], 'The May 16 Notification of 1966 marked its formal start.'),
         ('Which head of state died in detention in 1969 after being denounced as China’s “Khrushchev”?', 'Liu Shaoqi', ['Lin Biao', 'Zhou Enlai', 'Hua Guofeng'], 'Liu was expelled from the party in 1968 and died in Kaifeng.'),
         ('What happened to Lin Biao in September 1971?', 'He died in a plane crash in Mongolia after an alleged coup plot', ['He became chairman of the CCP', 'He led the Long March', 'He was executed after a public trial'], 'His death shattered faith in the Cultural Revolution.'),
         ('Mao’s big-character poster of August 1966 was titled:', '“Bombard the Headquarters”', ['“Serve the People”', '“Let a Hundred Flowers Bloom”', '“The East is Red”'], 'It was a direct attack on Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping.')],
  why=[('Explain why Mao launched the Cultural Revolution.', 'He wanted to regain power lost after the Great Leap, remove rivals such as Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, prevent “revisionism” like the USSR’s and renew revolutionary spirit among youth.'),
       ('Explain why the army was brought in to restore order in 1967–1968.', 'Red Guard factions fought each other, sometimes with weapons, and the economy and administration broke down; Mao relied on the PLA under Lin Biao to restore control.'),
       ('Explain why the Cultural Revolution shows the limits of totalitarian control.', 'Once mobilised, Red Guards pursued their own factional aims; local officials resisted; by the 1970s many peasants returned to private trade, showing that the state could not control all behaviour.')],
  contrast=[('the totalitarian model of the Cultural Revolution', 'revisionist views of the Cultural Revolution', 'The totalitarian model sees it as directed by Mao from above; revisionists stress local factions, social grievances and the chaos that escaped central control.')],
  misc=[('The Cultural Revolution was fully controlled by Mao at every stage.', 'Mao launched and steered it, but violence and factional conflict often escaped control, as in the Wuhan Incident of July 1967.'),
        ('The Cultural Revolution ended in 1969.', 'Although the most violent Red Guard phase ended in 1968–1969, the official Chinese view is that the Cultural Revolution lasted from 1966 until 1976.')],
  cases=[('the Wuhan Incident of July 1967', 'A conservative mass organisation and local army units detained two envoys from Beijing; Zhou Enlai had to intervene.',
          'the limits of central control during the Cultural Revolution', 'It showed that local army and factions could defy the centre, nearly leading to civil war.'),
         ('the destruction of the Confucius Temple at Qufu (November 1966)', 'Red Guards led by Tan Houlan smashed statues and graves in Confucius’ home town.',
          'the attack on the “Four Olds”', 'It shows the campaign’s attack on traditional culture and the power given to youth.')],
  src=[('Some leading comrades from the central down to the local levels have adopted the reactionary stand of the bourgeoisie, enforced a bourgeois dictatorship and struck down the surging movement of the great Cultural Revolution of the proletariat.',
        'Paraphrased from Mao Zedong, “Bombard the Headquarters – My First Big-Character Poster”, 5 August 1966',
        'Some senior party leaders are bourgeois revisionists who have suppressed the Cultural Revolution and must be attacked.',
        'Written by Mao himself, it shows that he directly targeted the party leadership.',
        'It does not name its targets openly and uses ideological language; it shows Mao’s propaganda, not the reality of the leaders’ views.'),
       ('Behind the façade of total control, people in the countryside quietly took back the land, ran black markets and ignored instructions. By the early 1970s a silent revolution from below was undermining the planned economy.',
        'Paraphrased from Frank Dikötter, Dutch historian, The Cultural Revolution: A People’s History, 1962–1976, 2016',
        'State control during the Cultural Revolution was weaker than it appeared, as ordinary people evaded it, especially in the countryside.',
        'Based on extensive research in provincial archives, providing evidence about everyday life.',
        'Dikötter is strongly critical of Mao; the emphasis on resistance from below may be overstated; access to archives in China was limited.')],
  cmp='Both refer to the conflict between Mao and others during the Cultural Revolution. Mao attacks senior leaders as bourgeois enemies, while Dikötter argues ordinary people quietly undermined state control; A shows Mao’s attack from above, B resistance from below.',
  ev=[('The Cultural Revolution demonstrates that Mao’s China was a totalitarian state.',
       ['Mao cult and mobilisation of millions', 'purge of Liu Shaoqi and Deng', 'destruction of culture and Four Olds'],
       ['factional violence escaped control', 'army needed to restore order', 'silent resistance in the countryside (Dikötter)'],
       'The Cultural Revolution shows totalitarian ambitions and power to mobilise, but also the limits of control in practice.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did Mao achieve his aims in the Cultural Revolution?',
           ['removal of Liu and Deng', 'revival of revolutionary spirit', 'chaos and army intervention', 'Lin Biao affair', 'death of Mao and arrest of the Gang of Four'],
           'Mao achieved short-term political aims, but failed to create lasting revolutionary renewal.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch11 Authoritarian Rule – Comparative Case Studies and Historiography
# =============================================================================================
H('ch11-st1', 'the emergence and rule of Fidel Castro in Cuba',
  terms=[('26 July Movement', 'Castro’s revolutionary movement, named after the failed attack on the Moncada barracks on 26 July 1953'),
         ('Committees for the Defence of the Revolution (CDRs)', 'neighbourhood committees founded in September 1960 to watch for counter-revolutionary activity'),
         ('Agrarian Reform Law (1959)', 'the law of May 1959 that limited landholdings and expropriated large estates, including US-owned sugar plantations'),
         ('UMAP camps', 'Military Units to Aid Production (1965–1968), labour camps for people seen as unfit for military service, including religious believers and gay men')],
  facts=[('On what date did Fulgencio Batista flee Cuba?', '1 January 1959', ['26 July 1953', '17 April 1961', '2 December 1956'], 'Batista left in the early hours of New Year’s Day.'),
         ('What was the Granma?', 'The yacht that carried Castro and 81 other rebels from Mexico to Cuba in 1956', ['A Cuban newspaper only', 'The ship sunk at the Bay of Pigs', 'A Soviet missile ship'], 'It landed on 2 December 1956; the official party newspaper was later named after it.'),
         ('In which year did Cuba carry out its national literacy campaign?', '1961', ['1953', '1970', '1980'], 'Volunteers, many teenagers, taught hundreds of thousands to read and write.'),
         ('The Mariel boatlift of 1980 involved approximately how many Cubans leaving for the USA?', 'About 125,000', ['About 1,200', 'About 1.25 million', 'About 12,000'], 'Castro allowed departures from Mariel harbour.')],
  why=[('Explain why Castro was able to overthrow Batista.', 'Batista’s regime was corrupt and repressive and lost middle-class support; the guerrillas in the Sierra Maestra won peasant support and media attention; the USA imposed an arms embargo in 1958 and the army collapsed.'),
       ('Explain why Castro moved closer to the Soviet Union in 1960–1961.', 'US hostility to land reform and nationalisation, the US sugar quota cut and embargo, and the Bay of Pigs invasion pushed Cuba to seek Soviet trade and protection, consistent with Castro’s radical aims.'),
       ('Explain why the CDRs were important in consolidating Castro’s rule.', 'They mobilised hundreds of thousands to monitor neighbours, distribute rations and organise campaigns, extending surveillance and participation into every street.')],
  contrast=[('Castro’s rise to power', 'Mussolini’s rise to power', 'Castro came to power through a guerrilla war and the collapse of the old regime; Mussolini through intimidation and appointment by the king within a constitutional framework.')],
  misc=[('Castro came to power as an openly communist leader.', 'In 1959 Castro presented himself as a nationalist reformer; he declared the revolution socialist only in April 1961, and the PSP communists initially distrusted him.'),
        ('Castro’s regime relied only on repression.', 'It also won support through land reform, literacy, free health care and nationalism, alongside the repression of opponents.')],
  cases=[('the literacy campaign of 1961', 'About 250,000 volunteers, including 100,000 young brigadistas, taught over 700,000 people to read; literacy rose to about 96%.',
          'the use of social policy to build support', 'It improved lives and mobilised youth for the revolution, while spreading revolutionary ideology.'),
         ('the Padilla affair of 1971', 'The poet Heberto Padilla was arrested and forced to make a public self-criticism, alienating many foreign intellectuals.',
          'the control of culture in Castro’s Cuba', 'It demonstrated the limits of artistic freedom set out in “Words to the Intellectuals”.')],
  src=[('I know that imprisonment will be harder for me than it has ever been for anyone. But I do not fear prison, as I do not fear the fury of the miserable tyrant who took the lives of my comrades. Condemn me. It does not matter. History will absolve me.',
        'Fidel Castro, closing words of his defence speech at his trial for the Moncada attack, Santiago de Cuba, 16 October 1953 (extract; the speech was later rewritten and published as a pamphlet)',
        'Castro refuses to fear imprisonment or Batista and claims that history will justify his actions.',
        'It shows Castro’s early self-presentation and the programme he used to build support after Moncada.',
        'The published version was reconstructed later in prison and used as propaganda, so it may differ from what he said at the trial.'),
       ('Within the Revolution, everything; against the Revolution, nothing. The Revolution has rights too, and the first right of the Revolution is to exist.',
        'Fidel Castro, “Words to the Intellectuals”, speech at the National Library, Havana, June 1961 (extract, translation)',
        'Artists and writers are free within the Revolution but cannot oppose it, because the Revolution’s right to survive comes first.',
        'A key statement of cultural policy by Castro himself, given to writers and artists.',
        'It is deliberately ambiguous and does not show how the boundary was enforced in practice, e.g. later in the Padilla affair.')],
  cmp='Both show Castro presenting the revolution as morally justified. In 1953 he presents himself as a victim appealing to history against tyranny, whereas in 1961 he speaks as the leader of a state setting limits on freedom; A defends rebellion, B restricts dissent.',
  ev=[('Castro maintained power in Cuba mainly through popular social reforms.',
       ['literacy campaign and free health care', 'land reform', 'nationalism and anti-US feeling'],
       ['CDRs and repression of opponents', 'emigration of opponents (Mariel)', 'Soviet economic support until 1991'],
       'Social reform built genuine support, but surveillance, repression and Soviet aid were also essential.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the reasons for Castro’s rise to power in Cuba.',
           ['weakness and corruption of Batista', 'guerrilla strategy and Sierra Maestra', 'role of the USA', 'Castro’s leadership and propaganda'],
           'Batista’s weakness was crucial, but Castro’s leadership turned it into revolutionary victory.')],
)

H('ch11-st2', 'comparisons between authoritarian states in different regions',
  terms=[('comparative history', 'the study of two or more cases to identify similarities and differences and test explanations'),
         ('totalitarian syndrome', 'Friedrich and Brzezinski’s six features of totalitarian states: an official ideology, a single mass party, terror, a monopoly of communications, a monopoly of weapons and a centrally directed economy'),
         ('polycracy', 'a system of competing power centres, used to describe the Nazi state in which agencies overlapped and competed')],
  facts=[('Which of the following leaders came to power through a guerrilla war?', 'Fidel Castro', ['Adolf Hitler', 'Benito Mussolini', 'Juan Perón'], 'Castro’s 26 July Movement fought from the Sierra Maestra in 1956–1958.'),
         ('Which of the following was a feature of Friedrich and Brzezinski’s model of totalitarianism (1956)?', 'A monopoly of mass communications', ['Free multi-party elections', 'An independent judiciary', 'A market economy without planning'], 'The model lists six features, including a monopoly of communications.')],
  why=[('Explain why comparing authoritarian states can help historians understand them.', 'Comparison identifies common conditions and methods (crisis, propaganda, terror) and distinctive features (ideology, social base), testing whether explanations apply beyond one case.'),
       ('Explain why historians have criticised the totalitarian model.', 'It focuses on the regime’s claims rather than reality, under-estimates chaos, competition and popular agency, and was shaped by Cold War politics that equated communism with Nazism.'),
       ('Explain why Fascist Italy is often seen as less totalitarian than Nazi Germany.', 'The monarchy, the Church and much of the old elite remained independent; terror was more limited; and Mussolini never controlled the army fully, as shown by his dismissal by the king in July 1943.')],
  contrast=[('the social base of Nazism', 'the social base of Maoism', 'Nazism drew much support from the middle classes, farmers and Protestant voters in an industrial society; Maoism drew its strength from the peasantry in an agrarian society.'),
            ('Hitler’s and Mao’s use of terror', 'Mussolini’s and Castro’s use of terror', 'Hitler and Mao used terror on a mass scale causing millions of deaths; Mussolini and Castro relied on more limited repression, internal exile, imprisonment and emigration.')],
  misc=[('All authoritarian states were essentially the same.', 'They shared methods such as propaganda and repression, but differed in ideology, social base, economic policy and the scale of violence.'),
        ('Comparison means describing two regimes one after the other.', 'Effective comparison analyses similarities and differences point by point, explaining why they existed.')],
  cases=[('the dismissal of Mussolini by the Fascist Grand Council and the king (24–25 July 1943)', 'After the Allied invasion of Sicily, the Grand Council voted against him and Victor Emmanuel III had him arrested.',
          'the limits of authoritarian power in comparative perspective', 'No such institution could remove Hitler, showing that Mussolini’s power was more limited than Hitler’s or Mao’s.')],
  src=[('The totalitarian dictatorships all share six features: an official ideology, a single mass party led by one man, a system of terror, a monopoly of mass communications, a monopoly of weapons, and central control of the economy.',
        'Paraphrased from Carl J. Friedrich and Zbigniew Brzezinski, US political scientists, Totalitarian Dictatorship and Autocracy, 1956',
        'Totalitarian regimes can be identified by six shared features of control.',
        'An influential model that provides clear criteria for comparison.',
        'Written during the Cold War, partly to equate Soviet communism with Nazism; it describes aims and structures rather than how regimes worked in practice.'),
       ('Comparing Hitler and Stalin shows as many differences as similarities. Stalin’s regime rested on a bureaucratic party apparatus; Hitler’s was a chaotic personal rule in which party and state agencies competed, held together by the Führer’s charisma.',
        'Paraphrased from Ian Kershaw and Moshe Lewin (eds), Stalinism and Nazism: Dictatorships in Comparison, 1997',
        'Nazi and Soviet regimes differed significantly in structure: bureaucratic party rule versus chaotic charismatic rule.',
        'Written by leading experts after the opening of Soviet archives, it offers nuanced comparative analysis.',
        'It focuses on two regimes; its conclusions may not apply to other authoritarian states such as Mao’s China or Castro’s Cuba.')],
  cmp='Both compare authoritarian regimes. Friedrich and Brzezinski stress shared features of totalitarian control, while Kershaw and Lewin emphasise structural differences between the Nazi and Soviet systems; A generalises, B differentiates.',
  ev=[('The similarities between authoritarian states are more significant than their differences.',
       ['single party, leader cult, propaganda and terror in all', 'control of youth and media', 'crisis conditions for emergence'],
       ['different ideologies and social bases', 'scale of terror varied greatly', 'different relations with traditional elites and churches'],
       'Methods were similar, but differences in ideology, aims and outcomes are historically more significant.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the methods used by two authoritarian leaders, each from a different region, to maintain power.',
           ['propaganda and leader cult', 'terror and repression', 'social and economic policies', 'foreign policy'],
           'Both relied on a combination of consent and coercion, but the balance differed according to context.')],
)

H('ch11-st3', 'the intentionalist–structuralist debate on the Nazi state and the Holocaust',
  terms=[('intentionalism', 'the interpretation that Hitler’s long-held ideological intentions and personal decisions drove Nazi policy, including the Holocaust'),
         ('structuralism (functionalism)', 'the interpretation that Nazi policy resulted from the chaotic structure of the regime, competition between agencies and improvisation'),
         ('cumulative radicalisation', 'Hans Mommsen’s term for the process by which competition within the Nazi state pushed policies towards ever more extreme measures'),
         ('working towards the Führer', 'Ian Kershaw’s synthesis, from a 1934 speech by Werner Willikens, that officials anticipated Hitler’s wishes and radicalised policy without direct orders')],
  facts=[('Which historian described Hitler as a “weak dictator”?', 'Hans Mommsen', ['Lucy Dawidowicz', 'Eberhard Jäckel', 'Daniel Goldhagen'], 'Mommsen argued that Hitler avoided decisions and was a prisoner of the system.'),
         ('Who wrote “The War Against the Jews, 1933–1945” (1975), a classic intentionalist work?', 'Lucy Dawidowicz', ['Martin Broszat', 'Christopher Browning', 'Raul Hilberg'], 'She argued Hitler had planned the destruction of the Jews from early on.'),
         ('Which historian wrote “Der Staat Hitlers” (The Hitler State, 1969), a key structuralist work?', 'Martin Broszat', ['Klaus Hildebrand', 'Ian Kershaw', 'A. J. P. Taylor'], 'Broszat analysed the Nazi state as polycratic.'),
         ('Which civil servant’s 1934 speech gave Kershaw the phrase “working towards the Führer”?', 'Werner Willikens', ['Reinhard Heydrich', 'Adolf Eichmann', 'Wilhelm Frick'], 'Willikens was a state secretary in the Prussian Agriculture Ministry.')],
  why=[('Explain why the absence of a written order from Hitler for the Holocaust is important in the debate.', 'Structuralists see it as evidence of improvisation and initiative from below; intentionalists argue Hitler gave verbal orders and his intentions were clear from his speeches, such as the “prophecy” of January 1939.'),
       ('Explain why Kershaw’s concept of “working towards the Führer” is seen as a synthesis.', 'It accepts Hitler’s ideological centrality (intentionalist) while explaining how competing agencies radicalised policy (structuralist) by anticipating his wishes.'),
       ('Explain why the context of historians shaped the intentionalist–structuralist debate.', 'West German historians in the 1960s–1980s examined how German institutions and society enabled Nazism, while some feared structuralism could diminish personal responsibility; the debate reflected wider arguments about German history.')],
  contrast=[('the intentionalist interpretation', 'the structuralist interpretation', 'Intentionalists explain Nazi policy through Hitler’s ideology and plans; structuralists through the regime’s chaotic institutions, competition and improvisation.')],
  misc=[('Structuralist historians argue that Hitler was unimportant.', 'Structuralists accept that Hitler’s ideology and authority were essential but argue that decisions emerged from the system rather than detailed plans.'),
        ('The intentionalist–structuralist debate is about whether the Holocaust happened.', 'Both schools fully accept the Holocaust; they disagree about how and why decisions were made.')],
  cases=[('Hitler’s Reichstag speech of 30 January 1939', 'Hitler “prophesied” that a new world war would result in “the annihilation of the Jewish race in Europe”.',
          'the intentionalist argument', 'Intentionalists see it as evidence of a long-held aim; structuralists see it as rhetoric that became policy only in wartime conditions.'),
         ('Kristallnacht (November 1938)', 'Goebbels launched the pogrom partly to regain Hitler’s favour; Göring and Himmler disliked the disorder and economic damage.',
          'polycratic competition in the Nazi state', 'It illustrates how rivalry between leaders could drive radical action, supporting the structuralist view.')],
  src=[('The Final Solution grew out of Hitler’s ideas; it was his intention from the start. He had laid out his programme in Mein Kampf, and he carried it out when war gave him the opportunity.',
        'Paraphrased from Lucy Dawidowicz, US historian, The War Against the Jews, 1933–1945, 1975',
        'The Holocaust was Hitler’s long-planned aim, set out in Mein Kampf and implemented when war allowed.',
        'A pioneering, influential study by a historian with deep knowledge of Jewish sources.',
        'Written before much archival research on decision-making; critics argue it reads history backwards from the outcome.'),
       ('It is the duty of everybody to try to work towards the Führer along the lines he would wish. Whoever works correctly towards the Führer along his lines and towards his goal will in future have the finest reward.',
        'Werner Willikens, State Secretary in the Prussian Ministry of Agriculture, speech to officials, 21 February 1934 (extract, translation)',
        'Officials should anticipate and carry out Hitler’s wishes and will be rewarded for doing so.',
        'A contemporary statement from inside the regime, showing how officials understood their role.',
        'It is a single speech to agricultural officials; it describes expectations, not how the Holocaust was decided.')],
  cmp='Both place Hitler at the centre of policy. Dawidowicz argues that Hitler planned the Holocaust from the start, whereas Willikens shows officials acting on Hitler’s presumed wishes rather than specific orders; A suggests a top-down plan, B a system of anticipation from below.',
  ev=[('The Holocaust was the result of Hitler’s long-term intentions rather than the structure of the Nazi state.',
       ['Mein Kampf antisemitism', 'January 1939 prophecy', 'Hitler’s approval of key decisions in 1941'],
       ['no written order; improvisation in the East', 'competing agencies (SS, Göring, Goebbels)', 'cumulative radicalisation (Mommsen)', 'Kershaw’s synthesis'],
       'Hitler’s intentions were essential, but the timing and form of the Holocaust emerged through the dynamics of the Nazi state in wartime.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast intentionalist and structuralist interpretations of the Nazi state.',
           ['Dawidowicz, Jäckel, Hildebrand', 'Broszat, Mommsen', 'Kershaw’s synthesis', 'evidence: decrees, speeches, Wannsee'],
           'Both have strengths; most historians now combine Hitler’s ideological intentions with the structural dynamics of the regime.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch12 Concept and Thematic Essay Writing (Paper 2)
# =============================================================================================
H('ch12-st1', 'the causes of the Second World War in Europe',
  terms=[('evaluate', 'make an appraisal by weighing up the strengths and limitations of an argument or factor'),
         ('to what extent', 'consider the merits or otherwise of an argument or concept, presenting a clear conclusion supported by evidence and sound argument'),
         ('compare and contrast', 'give an account of the similarities and differences between two or more items or situations, referring to both throughout'),
         ('discuss', 'offer a considered and balanced review that includes a range of arguments, factors or hypotheses, with conclusions supported by evidence'),
         ('examine', 'consider an argument or concept in a way that uncovers its assumptions and interrelationships')],
  facts=[('Which command term requires a candidate to refer to both cases throughout, identifying similarities and differences?', 'Compare and contrast', ['Discuss', 'Evaluate', 'Examine'], 'Compare and contrast requires similarities and differences, referring to both throughout.'),
         ('A question begins “To what extent…”. What must the answer include?', 'A clear judgement on how far the claim is valid, supported by evidence', ['A narrative of events in chronological order', 'Only evidence that supports the claim', 'A list of all possible causes without judgement'], 'It requires weighing the merits of the claim and reaching a supported conclusion.'),
         ('Which historian argued in “The Origins of the Second World War” (1961) that Hitler was an opportunist rather than a planner?', 'A. J. P. Taylor', ['Hugh Trevor-Roper', 'Fritz Fischer', 'Richard Overy'], 'Taylor’s thesis caused a major controversy.')],
  why=[('Explain why it is important to identify the command term before planning an essay.', 'The command term defines the task: “evaluate” requires weighing strengths and limitations, “compare and contrast” requires both cases throughout; misreading it leads to answers that do not meet the demands of the question.'),
       ('Explain why a “discuss” question still requires a conclusion.', 'Discussion must be balanced, but IB markbands reward a reasoned conclusion that follows from the arguments and evidence; without it the answer remains descriptive.'),
       ('Explain why narrative answers score poorly in IB History essays.', 'Narrative describes what happened without analysing why or how far; markbands place mainly narrative answers in the lower bands even if knowledge is accurate.')],
  contrast=[('“evaluate”', '“to what extent”', 'Both require judgement; “evaluate” asks for the strengths and limitations of something to be weighed, while “to what extent” asks how far a claim is valid and requires a clear position on the degree.'),
            ('“discuss”', '“examine”', '“Discuss” asks for a balanced review of arguments with a conclusion; “examine” asks the candidate to uncover assumptions and interrelationships of an issue.')],
  misc=[('In a compare-and-contrast essay you should write about the first case and then the second.', 'Effective answers use an integrated structure, comparing the cases point by point throughout the essay.'),
        ('An “evaluate” essay only needs to list the strengths of a factor.', 'Evaluation requires weighing strengths and limitations and reaching a judgement.')],
  cases=[('the essay question “To what extent was appeasement responsible for the outbreak of the Second World War in Europe?”', 'A strong answer weighs appeasement against Hitler’s aims, the Nazi–Soviet Pact, the failure of the League and the Versailles settlement.',
          'the demands of a “to what extent” question', 'It requires assessing the stated factor against others and concluding how far it was responsible, not simply narrating the 1930s.')],
  src=[('Hitler was no more wicked and unscrupulous than many other contemporary statesmen. In principle and doctrine, he was no more wicked than other German leaders. He was a traditional German statesman, exploiting the opportunities offered to him by others.',
        'Paraphrased from A. J. P. Taylor, British historian, The Origins of the Second World War, 1961',
        'Hitler was an opportunist pursuing traditional German aims, exploiting chances given by other powers, rather than a uniquely wicked planner of war.',
        'A highly influential, provocative interpretation based on diplomatic documents.',
        'Taylor relied mainly on published diplomatic papers and gave little weight to Hitler’s ideology; his provocative style may exaggerate.'),
       ('Hitler’s war aims were consistent: the conquest of living space in the East, as set out in Mein Kampf and repeated in the Hossbach Memorandum of 1937. To call him an opportunist ignores his programme.',
        'Paraphrased from Hugh Trevor-Roper, British historian, “A. J. P. Taylor, Hitler and the War”, Encounter, July 1961',
        'Hitler had consistent long-term aims of eastern conquest, so he was not simply an opportunist.',
        'A direct rejoinder by a leading historian of Nazi Germany using ideological evidence.',
        'It is a polemical review aimed at refuting Taylor, so it may underplay the role of opportunism and of other powers.')],
  cmp='Both focus on Hitler’s role in causing the war. Taylor sees him as a traditional opportunist exploiting others’ mistakes, while Trevor-Roper sees him as pursuing a consistent programme of conquest; one minimises Hitler’s unique responsibility, the other stresses it.',
  ev=[('Hitler’s foreign policy aims were the main cause of the Second World War in Europe.',
       ['Lebensraum in Mein Kampf', 'Hossbach Memorandum 1937', 'invasion of Poland 1939'],
       ['appeasement encouraged him (Taylor)', 'Versailles and the failure of the League', 'Nazi–Soviet Pact removed the risk of a two-front war'],
       'Hitler’s aims were the main cause, but other powers’ policies shaped the timing and form of the war.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was appeasement responsible for the outbreak of the Second World War in Europe?',
           ['Rhineland, Anschluss, Munich', 'Hitler’s aims and Lebensraum', 'Nazi–Soviet Pact', 'Versailles and the League'],
           'Appeasement encouraged Hitler but did not cause war; responsibility lies mainly with Hitler’s expansionist aims.'),
          ('Discuss the view that the Second World War in Europe was “Hitler’s war”.',
           ['intentionalist view of foreign policy', 'Taylor’s opportunism thesis', 'role of Britain, France and the USSR', 'structural causes from 1919'],
           'The war was primarily Hitler’s, though others’ decisions made it possible.')],
)

H('ch12-st2', 'the reasons for Mussolini’s rise to power',
  terms=[('thesis', 'the central argument of an essay, stated clearly in the introduction and developed throughout'),
         ('counter-argument', 'a view that challenges the thesis, which a strong essay acknowledges and evaluates'),
         ('judgement', 'a reasoned conclusion on the question, following from the analysis of evidence'),
         ('topic sentence', 'the opening sentence of a paragraph stating the analytical point the paragraph will prove')],
  facts=[('Where should the thesis of an IB History essay usually be stated?', 'In the introduction, and sustained throughout', ['Only in the conclusion', 'In a footnote', 'In the middle paragraph'], 'Markbands reward focused, argument-led essays.'),
         ('Which of the following is the best topic sentence for an analytical paragraph?', 'The weakness of the liberal state was the most important reason for Mussolini’s appointment in 1922.',
          ['Mussolini was born in 1883 in Predappio.', 'In 1919 many things happened in Italy.', 'This paragraph will be about the king.'], 'A topic sentence states an analytical point linked to the question.'),
         ('In which year was Mussolini appointed prime minister of Italy?', '1922', ['1919', '1925', '1929'], 'He was appointed on 30 October 1922 after the March on Rome.')],
  why=[('Explain why a strong essay includes counter-arguments.', 'Engaging with counter-arguments shows awareness of different perspectives and makes the final judgement more convincing; top markbands require evaluation of different perspectives.'),
       ('Explain why planning is essential in a timed Paper 2 essay.', 'A short plan identifies the thesis, the main factors and evidence and the judgement, ensuring the essay stays focused and structured instead of drifting into narrative.'),
       ('Explain why the conclusion should not introduce new evidence.', 'The conclusion should bring together the argument and give a reasoned judgement; new evidence belongs in the body where it can be analysed.')],
  contrast=[('a thesis-led essay', 'a narrative essay', 'A thesis-led essay argues a position in each paragraph and reaches a judgement; a narrative essay tells events in order without analysis.')],
  misc=[('A balanced essay must give exactly equal space to each side and avoid taking a position.', 'Balance means considering different views fairly; the essay must still reach a clear, supported judgement.'),
        ('The more facts an essay includes, the higher its mark.', 'Knowledge must be relevant and used to support analysis; unfocused detail does not raise the band.')],
  cases=[('a plan for the question “To what extent was the weakness of the liberal state responsible for Mussolini’s rise to power?”', 'Thesis: weakness was the key condition but Fascist violence and elite support were decisive; paragraphs on liberal weakness, socio-economic crisis, Fascist methods and the king.',
          'the structure of a thesis-led essay with counter-argument', 'The plan sets out a clear line of argument and places counter-arguments within a structure leading to a judgement.')],
  src=[('Fascism did not conquer power; it was handed to it. The king refused to sign the decree of martial law, and the army, which could have dispersed the marchers in hours, was never ordered to act.',
        'Paraphrased from Adrian Lyttelton, British historian, The Seizure of Power: Fascism in Italy 1919–1929, 1973',
        'Mussolini did not seize power by force; the king and army allowed him to take office by not resisting.',
        'An authoritative study of Fascism’s rise based on Italian archives.',
        'It focuses on the political elite and may give less weight to the mass appeal of Fascism.'),
       ('Fascism attracted genuine support among the middle classes, war veterans and students who saw it as a movement of national renewal against socialism and the discredited liberal politicians.',
        'Paraphrased from Emilio Gentile, Italian historian, The Origins of Fascist Ideology 1918–1925, 1975',
        'Fascism had real popular support from groups seeking national renewal and opposing socialism.',
        'Written by a leading Italian historian of Fascist ideology, it offers insight into the movement’s appeal.',
        'Its focus on ideology and culture may underplay the role of violence and elite intrigue.')],
  cmp='Both explain why Mussolini gained power. Lyttelton stresses that power was handed to him by the king and army, while Gentile emphasises genuine support for Fascism among the middle classes and veterans; one focuses on elites, the other on popular appeal.',
  ev=[('The weakness of the liberal state was the main reason for Mussolini’s rise to power.',
       ['trasformismo and unstable coalitions', 'failure to deal with the biennio rosso', 'king’s refusal to declare martial law'],
       ['squadristi violence', 'Fascist appeal to the middle class and veterans', 'support from industrialists and landowners', 'Mussolini’s leadership'],
       'Liberal weakness was the essential precondition, but Fascist violence and elite support turned it into Mussolini’s appointment.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was the weakness of the liberal state responsible for Mussolini’s rise to power by 1922?',
           ['liberal weakness and trasformismo', 'socio-economic crisis', 'Fascist violence and propaganda', 'king and elites'],
           'Liberal weakness was necessary but not sufficient; elite collaboration was decisive.')],
)

H('ch12-st3', 'the rise of authoritarian leaders in different regions',
  terms=[('thematic essay', 'a Paper 2 essay on a theme such as authoritarian states, often requiring examples from two different regions'),
         ('region', 'one of the IB regions (Africa and the Middle East, the Americas, Asia and Oceania, Europe) used to organise examples'),
         ('integrated comparison', 'analysis that discusses two cases together within each paragraph rather than separately')],
  facts=[('If a Paper 2 question asks for examples “each chosen from a different region”, which pair would be acceptable?', 'Hitler (Europe) and Castro (the Americas)', ['Hitler and Mussolini', 'Mao and Deng', 'Stalin and Hitler'], 'Hitler and Castro are from different IB regions; Hitler, Mussolini and Stalin are all European.'),
         ('Which of the following is an example of an authoritarian leader from the Americas?', 'Juan Perón', ['Kemal Atatürk', 'Mao Zedong', 'Benito Mussolini'], 'Perón ruled Argentina 1946–1955 and 1973–1974.')],
  why=[('Explain why it is important to use specific evidence from both regions in a thematic essay.', 'The question tests comparative understanding; general statements or a focus on one case cannot show how the theme applies across regions and limit the mark.'),
       ('Explain why integrated comparison is stronger than separate accounts.', 'It makes similarities and differences explicit point by point, showing analysis rather than leaving the examiner to infer the comparison.'),
       ('Explain why choosing well-matched examples helps in a thematic essay.', 'Cases with comparable features (e.g. Hitler and Castro on use of force) allow meaningful comparison, while badly matched cases lead to superficial contrasts.')],
  contrast=[('Hitler’s use of legal methods', 'Castro’s use of guerrilla warfare', 'Hitler gained power through elections and appointment within the constitution; Castro won through armed struggle and the collapse of Batista’s regime.')],
  misc=[('Any two authoritarian leaders can be used for a question requiring two regions.', 'The examples must come from different IB regions; Hitler and Mussolini are both European.'),
        ('A thematic essay should spend most of its length on background.', 'Background should be brief; the essay must focus on analysis of the theme across both cases.')],
  cases=[('a comparison of Hitler and Castro on the use of force', 'Hitler used the threat of SA violence while pursuing legality; Castro relied on guerrilla war from the Sierra Maestra and executions of Batista officials after victory.',
          'the effective use of examples from two regions in a thematic essay', 'The comparison shows that both used force but in different ways and at different stages, supporting a nuanced judgement.')],
  src=[('[Photograph] Fidel Castro, rifle on his shoulder, addresses a huge crowd in Havana, January 1959; banners read “Viva Fidel” and “Libertad”.',
        'Photograph of Fidel Castro’s arrival in Havana, 8 January 1959, published internationally (description)',
        'Castro was greeted as a popular hero and liberator, combining military power with mass support.',
        'It shows the scale of popular enthusiasm at the moment of victory.',
        'A single image chosen for publication; it does not show opponents or later disillusionment.'),
       ('[Photograph] Hitler, in a suit, bows and shakes hands with President Hindenburg, in military uniform, at the Garrison Church in Potsdam, 21 March 1933.',
        'Photograph of the “Day of Potsdam”, 21 March 1933, widely reproduced in Nazi propaganda (description)',
        'Hitler presents himself as the respectful heir of Prussian tradition, gaining legitimacy from the old elites represented by Hindenburg.',
        'It shows how the Nazis used ceremony to present the seizure of power as legal and traditional.',
        'It was staged and distributed by Nazi propaganda; it conceals the violence occurring at the same time.')],
  cmp='Both images show new leaders seeking legitimacy in 1933 and 1959. Castro’s legitimacy comes from mass popular support and his armed victory, whereas Hitler’s comes from the endorsement of the traditional elite; one is revolutionary, the other presented as traditional and legal.',
  ev=[('Authoritarian leaders came to power mainly through the use of force.',
       ['Castro’s guerrilla war', 'Mao’s civil war', 'SA and squadristi violence'],
       ['Hitler’s and Mussolini’s appointment', 'propaganda and elections', 'elite collaboration'],
       'Force was essential in some cases (Castro, Mao), but legal methods and elite collaboration were decisive in others (Hitler, Mussolini).')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the rise to power of two authoritarian leaders, each from a different region.',
           ['conditions: crisis in both', 'methods: legal and violent', 'role of ideology', 'role of leadership'],
           'Both exploited crisis, but methods differed according to political context.'),
          ('Evaluate the importance of the use of force in the rise to power of two authoritarian leaders, each chosen from a different region.',
           ['Castro: decisive guerrilla war', 'Hitler: threat of force within legality', 'other factors: propaganda, elites', 'judgement'],
           'Force was decisive for Castro but secondary to legal methods for Hitler.')],
)

H('ch12-st4', 'the international response to the conflicts in Rwanda and Kosovo',
  terms=[('concept-based question', 'an essay question framed around one of the six key concepts, such as significance or consequence, applied to a focused study'),
         ('significance', 'in an essay, the importance of an event judged by its depth, breadth and durability of impact'),
         ('responsibility to protect', 'the principle that the international community has a duty to protect populations from genocide and mass atrocities when their state fails to do so')],
  facts=[('A question asks “Evaluate the significance of the Arusha Accords for the outbreak of the genocide in Rwanda.” Which key concept is the focus?', 'Significance', ['Continuity', 'Change', 'Perspectives'], 'The question explicitly asks about significance.'),
         ('Which UN Secretary-General asked in 2000 how the world should respond “to a Rwanda, to a Srebrenica”?', 'Kofi Annan', ['Boutros Boutros-Ghali', 'Ban Ki-moon', 'Javier Pérez de Cuéllar'], 'Annan’s Millennium Report challenged states to reconcile sovereignty and intervention.')],
  why=[('Explain why a concept-based essay must define how the concept will be judged.', 'Concepts like significance or consequence need criteria (short- and long-term, depth, breadth) so that the essay can reach a measurable judgement rather than assert importance.'),
       ('Explain why using both focused studies can strengthen a concept-based essay.', 'Comparing Rwanda and Kosovo can show how the concept (e.g. consequence of international inaction or intervention) operated differently in each case, adding depth to the judgement.')],
  contrast=[('a concept-based question', 'a thematic question', 'A concept-based question asks the candidate to apply a key concept to a focused study; a thematic question asks about a theme (such as authoritarian states) across examples, often from different regions.')],
  misc=[('In a significance question it is enough to describe what happened.', 'The candidate must judge how and why the event mattered, using criteria and comparison with other factors.'),
        ('Significance can only be judged by short-term effects.', 'Significance must include long-term consequences and changing perspectives, e.g. the creation of the ICTR and R2P.')],
  cases=[('the question “Evaluate the consequences of international inaction during the genocide in Rwanda.”', 'A strong answer analyses the immediate consequences for victims, the refugee crisis and the Congo wars, the ICTR and the later development of R2P.',
          'the demands of a concept-based essay on consequence', 'It shows how the concept of consequence can be applied across short- and long-term effects to build a judgement.')],
  src=[('If humanitarian intervention is, indeed, an unacceptable assault on sovereignty, how should we respond to a Rwanda, to a Srebrenica – to gross and systematic violations of human rights that offend every precept of our common humanity?',
        'Kofi Annan, UN Secretary-General, “We the Peoples: The Role of the United Nations in the 21st Century” (Millennium Report), 2000 (extract)',
        'The international community cannot use sovereignty to excuse inaction in the face of atrocities like Rwanda and Srebrenica.',
        'A key statement by the UN Secretary-General reflecting on Rwanda and Kosovo, influential in the development of R2P.',
        'Annan had been head of UN peacekeeping in 1994 and was criticised for inaction, so he may wish to shift responsibility to member states.'),
       ('The bombing of Yugoslavia was presented as humanitarian, but the West ignored equally serious atrocities by its allies elsewhere. Humanitarian intervention is applied selectively by the powerful.',
        'Paraphrased from Noam Chomsky, US linguist and political critic, The New Military Humanism: Lessons from Kosovo, 1999',
        'Humanitarian intervention in Kosovo was selective and served the interests of powerful states.',
        'It represents a significant critical perspective on Western intervention.',
        'Chomsky is a polemical critic of US foreign policy, not a historian of the Balkans; written during the immediate aftermath without full evidence.')],
  cmp='Both raise questions about the international response to atrocities. Annan argues that sovereignty should not prevent intervention against atrocities, while Chomsky argues that intervention is selective and self-interested; Annan supports a principle of intervention, Chomsky questions its sincerity.',
  ev=[('The international response to Rwanda was more significant than the response to Kosovo in shaping later humanitarian intervention.',
       ['shame over Rwanda influenced Kosovo and R2P', 'Clinton apology and Carlsson report', 'ICTR set precedents'],
       ['Kosovo created the precedent of intervention without UN approval', 'Kosovo Commission’s “illegal but legitimate”', 'Blair doctrine'],
       'Rwanda provided the moral lesson and Kosovo the practical precedent; both shaped R2P.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the significance of international intervention, or its absence, in the outcome of the conflicts in Rwanda and Kosovo.',
           ['Rwanda: UNAMIR withdrawal and RPF victory', 'Turquoise', 'Kosovo: Allied Force and Resolution 1244', 'long-term consequences'],
           'Absence of intervention allowed genocide in Rwanda, while intervention decided the outcome in Kosovo, though with costs.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch13 Historiography and Historical Interpretation
# =============================================================================================
H('ch13-st1', 'different approaches to writing history',
  terms=[('Marxist historiography', 'an approach that explains history through class struggle and economic forces, e.g. E. P. Thompson and Eric Hobsbawm'),
         ('revisionism', 'the reinterpretation of an accepted historical view, often in response to new evidence or changing perspectives'),
         ('post-revisionism', 'an interpretation that synthesises orthodox and revisionist views, as in John Lewis Gaddis’s work on the Cold War'),
         ('Annales school', 'a French school of history, founded by Marc Bloch and Lucien Febvre in 1929, stressing long-term social and economic structures (the longue durée)'),
         ('Whig history', 'history written as a story of inevitable progress towards the present, criticised by Herbert Butterfield in 1931')],
  facts=[('Which historian wrote “The Making of the English Working Class” (1963)?', 'E. P. Thompson', ['A. J. P. Taylor', 'Leopold von Ranke', 'Fernand Braudel'], 'Thompson pioneered “history from below” within Marxist historiography.'),
         ('Which German historian famously stated that history should show “how it actually was” (wie es eigentlich gewesen)?', 'Leopold von Ranke', ['Karl Marx', 'Fritz Fischer', 'Hans Mommsen'], 'Ranke’s 1824 preface became the motto of source-based, “scientific” history.'),
         ('Which historian is associated with the concept of the longue durée?', 'Fernand Braudel', ['E. H. Carr', 'Eric Hobsbawm', 'Hugh Trevor-Roper'], 'Braudel’s study of the Mediterranean (1949) emphasised long-term structures.')],
  why=[('Explain why Marxist historians focus on economic and social structures.', 'Marxist theory sees the economic base and class relations as the driving forces of history, so explanations of events focus on class conflict and modes of production.'),
       ('Explain why revisionism is a normal part of historical writing.', 'New evidence, methods and questions lead historians to challenge accepted views; history is a continuing debate rather than a fixed account.'),
       ('Explain why “history from below” changed historical writing.', 'It shifted attention from elites to ordinary people, using new sources such as court records and oral history, and showed that ordinary people shaped events.')],
  contrast=[('orthodox interpretations', 'revisionist interpretations', 'Orthodox interpretations are the established view; revisionist interpretations challenge it, often using new evidence or perspectives.'),
            ('Marxist history', 'the Annales approach', 'Marxist history stresses class struggle and economic change; the Annales school stresses long-term geographical, social and mental structures without a single theory of class.')],
  misc=[('Revisionist historians are those who deny that events happened.', 'In historiography, revisionism means reinterpreting the past; denial (e.g. Holocaust denial) is a distortion of evidence, not legitimate revisionism.'),
        ('Ranke’s approach shows that history can be completely objective.', 'Ranke aimed for source-based accuracy, but his choice of subjects (states, diplomacy) reflected his own assumptions.')],
  cases=[('the Fischer controversy of the 1960s', 'Fritz Fischer’s argument that Germany sought world power in 1914 challenged the West German consensus and provoked bitter debate.',
          'revisionism in historiography', 'It shows how new archival evidence and a new generation’s perspective can overturn an orthodox view.')],
  src=[('I am seeking to rescue the poor stockinger, the Luddite cropper, the “obsolete” hand-loom weaver, the “utopian” artisan from the enormous condescension of posterity.',
        'E. P. Thompson, British Marxist historian, preface to The Making of the English Working Class, 1963 (extract)',
        'Historians should recover the experiences of ordinary working people whom history had dismissed.',
        'A founding statement of “history from below” by a leading historian.',
        'It reflects Thompson’s political commitment to the labour movement, which may shape his sympathy for his subjects.'),
       ('To history has been given the function of judging the past, of instructing men for the profit of future years. The present attempt does not aspire to such high offices. It wants only to show what actually happened.',
        'Leopold von Ranke, German historian, preface to Histories of the Latin and Germanic Nations from 1494 to 1514, 1824 (translation)',
        'History should not judge or teach lessons but should simply show what happened.',
        'A founding statement of modern source-based history.',
        'It expresses an ideal; Ranke’s own work focused on states and elites and reflected his conservative views.')],
  cmp='Both state an aim for historians. Thompson wants to recover the lives of ordinary people and challenge their neglect, while Ranke wants only to show what happened without judgement; Thompson is openly committed, Ranke claims neutrality.',
  ev=[('Marxist historiography has made the most important contribution to historical understanding.',
       ['history from below', 'economic and social explanations of revolutions', 'Hobsbawm’s Age of trilogy'],
       ['determinism and neglect of individuals', 'Annales and cultural history', 'decline after 1989'],
       'Marxism transformed social history, but no single school has a monopoly on insight.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the view that all history is revisionist.',
           ['Ranke and source-based history', 'Fischer controversy', 'Cold War schools', 'difference between revision and denial'],
           'History is inherently revisionist, though revision must remain disciplined by evidence.')],
)

H('ch13-st2', 'the motives of Holocaust perpetrators',
  terms=[('interpretation', 'a historian’s argument about the past, which can be evaluated by its evidence, logic and context'),
         ('Reserve Police Battalion 101', 'a unit of middle-aged Hamburg policemen who shot or deported tens of thousands of Jews in Poland in 1942–1943, studied by Christopher Browning'),
         ('eliminationist antisemitism', 'Daniel Goldhagen’s term for a deep-rooted German antisemitism that he argued made ordinary Germans willing killers')],
  facts=[('Who wrote “Ordinary Men: Reserve Police Battalion 101 and the Final Solution in Poland” (1992)?', 'Christopher Browning', ['Daniel Goldhagen', 'Raul Hilberg', 'Lucy Dawidowicz'], 'Browning used post-war interrogations of the battalion’s members.'),
         ('Which book argued in 1996 that ordinary Germans were “willing executioners” because of eliminationist antisemitism?', 'Hitler’s Willing Executioners', ['Ordinary Men', 'The Destruction of the European Jews', 'The War Against the Jews'], 'Daniel Goldhagen’s book sparked a major international debate.'),
         ('According to Browning, what happened when Major Trapp gave the men of Battalion 101 the option not to shoot at Józefów in July 1942?', 'Only about a dozen of around 500 men stepped out', ['All refused to shoot', 'Most refused to shoot', 'The battalion mutinied'], 'Browning stresses conformity and peer pressure.')],
  why=[('Explain why historians must evaluate an interpretation’s evidence, not just its conclusion.', 'An interpretation is only as strong as its evidence and reasoning; examining sources, sample and method reveals whether its conclusion is justified.'),
       ('Explain why Browning and Goldhagen reached different conclusions from similar evidence.', 'They asked different questions and interpreted the same interrogations differently: Browning stressed situational pressures, Goldhagen pre-existing German antisemitism.'),
       ('Explain why it is important to use historians’ interpretations in an IB essay.', 'Interpretations show awareness of debate and perspectives, but they must be used to support an argument, not simply listed.')],
  contrast=[('Browning’s interpretation', 'Goldhagen’s interpretation', 'Browning argues that ordinary men killed because of conformity, careerism and wartime brutalisation; Goldhagen argues they killed because of a uniquely German eliminationist antisemitism.')],
  misc=[('Quoting a historian’s name is enough to show understanding of historiography.', 'Credit comes from explaining and evaluating the interpretation and using it to support an argument.'),
        ('A more recent interpretation is always more accurate.', 'Recency does not guarantee quality; interpretations must be judged by evidence and reasoning.')],
  cases=[('the reception of Goldhagen’s book in Germany in 1996', 'Academic historians largely criticised it, but it was very popular with German audiences during his lecture tour.',
          'how interpretations are shaped and received', 'It shows the gap between professional and public perspectives and the role of national memory.')],
  src=[('The men of Reserve Police Battalion 101 were not chosen for their ideological commitment. Most killed not out of hatred but because they did not want to break ranks, to appear weak or to leave the dirty work to their comrades.',
        'Paraphrased from Christopher Browning, US historian, Ordinary Men: Reserve Police Battalion 101 and the Final Solution in Poland, 1992',
        'Ordinary men became killers mainly because of conformity and peer pressure rather than ideological hatred.',
        'Based on post-war judicial interrogations of 125 battalion members, it provides detailed evidence.',
        'The testimony was given in self-defence to prosecutors, so men may have minimised antisemitism; the battalion may not be typical.'),
       ('The perpetrators were ordinary Germans animated by antisemitism, by a particular type of antisemitism that led them to conclude that the Jews ought to die.',
        'Daniel Goldhagen, US political scientist, Hitler’s Willing Executioners: Ordinary Germans and the Holocaust, 1996 (adapted)',
        'Ordinary Germans killed Jews because of a deep-rooted antisemitism that made them believe Jews should die.',
        'A bold, much-debated thesis that drew attention to perpetrators’ beliefs.',
        'Widely criticised for selective use of evidence and for a mono-causal, national-character explanation; written as a provocative revision of Browning.')],
  cmp='Both study ordinary German perpetrators, partly the same battalion. Browning explains killing through conformity and situational pressures, whereas Goldhagen explains it through deep-rooted antisemitism; Browning’s explanation is situational and universal, Goldhagen’s ideological and specifically German.',
  ev=[('Situational pressures rather than ideology explain why ordinary men took part in the Holocaust.',
       ['Browning: conformity, peer pressure', 'Milgram experiments', 'few chose not to shoot'],
       ['Goldhagen: eliminationist antisemitism', 'years of propaganda', 'non-German collaborators also killed'],
       'Situational factors were crucial, but they worked in a context shaped by ideology and propaganda.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the contribution of Christopher Browning and Daniel Goldhagen to our understanding of Holocaust perpetrators.',
           ['Browning’s method and evidence', 'Goldhagen’s thesis and reception', 'criticism of each', 'later research'],
           'Browning’s nuanced interpretation is generally seen as more convincing, but Goldhagen focused attention on ideology.')],
)

H('ch13-st3', 'history, memory and commemoration of genocide',
  terms=[('collective memory', 'the shared, socially constructed understanding of the past held by a group or nation'),
         ('lieux de mémoire', 'Pierre Nora’s term for “sites of memory” – places, objects or rituals through which a community remembers its past'),
         ('Kwibuka', 'Kinyarwanda for “remember”; the annual commemoration of the genocide against the Tutsi, beginning on 7 April')],
  facts=[('In which year did the Kigali Genocide Memorial open?', '2004', ['1994', '1998', '2014'], 'It opened on the tenth anniversary; over 250,000 victims are buried there.'),
         ('Where is the main memorial to the victims of the July 1995 massacre in Bosnia?', 'Potočari, near Srebrenica', ['Sarajevo city centre', 'Belgrade', 'Pristina'], 'The Srebrenica–Potočari Memorial was dedicated in 2003.')],
  why=[('Explain why governments use commemoration to shape national identity.', 'Memorials and anniversaries present an official narrative of the past that defines who belongs to the nation and justifies current policies.'),
       ('Explain why history and memory can conflict.', 'Memory selects and simplifies the past to serve identity, while history seeks evidence-based complexity; for example, official Rwandan memory focuses on Tutsi victims and silences RPF crimes.')],
  contrast=[('history', 'memory', 'History is a critical, evidence-based reconstruction of the past; memory is a selective, emotional and identity-based recollection that can be shaped by politics.')],
  misc=[('Memorials provide a neutral record of what happened.', 'Memorials reflect choices about what to remember and how, often serving political and national purposes.'),
        ('Commemoration of the Kosovo war is the same in Serbia and Kosovo.', 'In Kosovo 1999 is commemorated as liberation; in Serbia the NATO bombing (from 24 March) is remembered as aggression and victimhood.')],
  cases=[('the Murambi Genocide Memorial', 'At a former technical school where tens of thousands were killed in April 1994, preserved bodies are displayed.',
          'the politics of genocide commemoration', 'Displaying remains forces confrontation with the genocide but raises ethical questions and serves the state’s narrative.')],
  src=[('Remembrance is not only about the past. We remember to ensure that such a thing never happens again, and to build a Rwanda in which we are all Rwandans.',
        'Paraphrased from Paul Kagame, President of Rwanda, speech at the 20th commemoration of the genocide (Kwibuka 20), Kigali, 7 April 2014',
        'Commemoration of the genocide serves to prevent repetition and to build national unity.',
        'A speech by the head of state at the major commemoration, showing the official purpose of memory.',
        'It presents the government’s narrative and political aims; it does not address criticisms about RPF crimes or restrictions on debate.'),
       ('Memory is life, always carried by living groups and in permanent evolution. History is the always incomplete reconstruction of what is no longer. Memory is a perpetually actual phenomenon; history is a representation of the past.',
        'Pierre Nora, French historian, “Between Memory and History: Les Lieux de Mémoire”, 1989 (translation)',
        'Memory is living and changing in groups, while history is a critical reconstruction of the past.',
        'An influential theoretical statement by the historian who developed the concept of sites of memory.',
        'It is abstract and based on French national memory; it does not analyse a specific case such as genocide.')],
  cmp='Both discuss the role of remembering the past. Kagame sees commemoration as a tool to prevent repetition and build unity, while Nora distinguishes memory from history and suggests that memory is living and changeable; one is political, the other theoretical.',
  ev=[('Official commemoration of genocide does more to serve political purposes than to preserve historical truth.',
       ['Rwandan state narrative and genocide ideology law', 'Serbian commemoration of NATO bombing', 'selective memory'],
       ['memorials preserve evidence and honour victims', 'educate against denial', 'survivors’ testimony'],
       'Commemoration serves both truth and politics; historians must analyse memorials as sources of both.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the relationship between history and memory, with reference to the commemoration of the Rwandan genocide.',
           ['Kwibuka and memorials', 'state narrative', 'silences in memory', 'historians’ role'],
           'Memory honours victims and shapes identity, but historians must examine it critically.')],
)


# =============================================================================================
# ch14 Historical Investigation (IA): Planning and Research
# =============================================================================================
H('ch14-st1', 'framing a focused historical research question',
  terms=[('research question', 'a focused, open question that the historical investigation sets out to answer'),
         ('scope', 'the limits of an investigation in time, place and subject, which keep it manageable'),
         ('historical investigation', 'the IB internal assessment in which a student investigates a historical question of their choice using sources')],
  facts=[('Which of the following is the best research question for an IB historical investigation?', 'To what extent did RTLM broadcasts influence the participation of civilians in killings in Kigali in April 1994?',
          ['What happened in Rwanda?', 'Was the Second World War bad?', 'Why did the Romans build roads, and how did the Cold War end?'], 'It is focused, open, historical and answerable within the word limit.'),
         ('Which of the following is the main purpose of the reflection section of the historical investigation?', 'To consider what the process revealed about the methods and challenges of historians',
          ['To summarise the conclusion of the investigation', 'To list every source consulted', 'To describe the background of the topic'], 'Reflection focuses on historical method, linked to the student’s own investigation.')],
  why=[('Explain why a narrow research question is more effective than a broad one.', 'A narrow question can be investigated in depth with specific sources within the word limit, allowing analysis rather than superficial coverage.'),
       ('Explain why a research question should be open rather than closed.', 'An open question (e.g. “To what extent…”) invites analysis and a judgement, while a closed question has a simple factual answer.'),
       ('Explain why availability of sources should influence the choice of topic.', 'Without accessible primary and secondary sources, the student cannot evaluate evidence or support an argument.')],
  contrast=[('an open research question', 'a closed research question', 'An open question, such as “How significant…?”, requires analysis and judgement; a closed question, such as “When did…?”, has a simple factual answer.')],
  misc=[('A good research question should cover as long a period as possible.', 'A focused period and topic allow depth; very broad questions lead to narrative and superficial treatment.'),
        ('Any interesting topic is suitable for the historical investigation.', 'The topic must be historical, researchable with available sources and answerable within the word limit.')],
  cases=[('refining a broad topic on Kosovo', '“Kosovo in the 1990s” was narrowed to “To what extent did the Dayton Agreement (1995) cause the shift from non-violent to armed resistance among Kosovo Albanians by 1998?”',
          'the process of framing a research question', 'The refined question is focused in time, place and issue and invites a supported judgement.')],
  src=[('Every step in the argument depends on asking a question. The historian does not wait for the evidence to speak; he puts the evidence to the question.',
        'Paraphrased from R. G. Collingwood, British philosopher and historian, The Idea of History, published 1946',
        'Historical knowledge is created by asking questions of evidence rather than waiting for it to reveal the past.',
        'A classic work on historical method by a philosopher-historian.',
        'It is philosophical and abstract; published after his death from lectures and essays, edited by T. M. Knox.'),
       ('Research begins with a problem, not a topic. A well-defined question guides the search for sources and prevents the historian from drowning in material.',
        'Paraphrased from John Tosh, British historian, The Pursuit of History, 1984 (later editions to 2021)',
        'Research should be driven by a precise question, which guides source selection and keeps research focused.',
        'A widely used guide to historical method by an experienced historian.',
        'It is a general methodological text, not evidence from a particular research project.')],
  cmp='Both stress the importance of questions in historical research. Collingwood presents questioning as the basis of all historical knowledge, while Tosh gives practical advice that a precise question guides the selection of sources; one is philosophical, the other practical.',
  ev=[('The research question is the most important factor in the success of a historical investigation.',
       ['focus and scope', 'determines source selection', 'enables argument'],
       ['quality of sources', 'analysis and evaluation', 'reflection on method'],
       'A strong question is necessary, but success also depends on analysis and evaluation of sources.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the importance of the research question in shaping a historical investigation.',
           ['focus and scope', 'sources and method', 'argument and judgement', 'reflection'],
           'The research question shapes the whole investigation, though it may be refined as research proceeds.')],
)

H('ch14-st2', 'identifying and evaluating sources for a historical investigation',
  terms=[('authenticity', 'whether a source is genuinely what it claims to be'),
         ('reliability', 'the extent to which a source’s information can be trusted for a given purpose'),
         ('oral history', 'evidence gathered through recorded interviews with people who experienced past events')],
  facts=[('In 1983 which German magazine published forged “Hitler Diaries”?', 'Stern', ['Der Spiegel', 'Die Zeit', 'Bild'], 'Forensic tests showed post-war materials; the forger was Konrad Kujau.'),
         ('Which British historian initially authenticated the Hitler Diaries before reversing his view?', 'Hugh Trevor-Roper', ['A. J. P. Taylor', 'Ian Kershaw', 'Richard Evans'], 'His reputation was damaged by the episode.'),
         ('Which of the following is the most important reason for choosing a source for detailed evaluation in the IA?', 'Its relevance and significance for the research question', ['Its length', 'That it agrees with the student’s argument', 'That it was the first source found'], 'Sources for detailed evaluation should be chosen for their importance to the investigation.')],
  why=[('Explain why it is important to evaluate sources in relation to the research question.', 'The same source can be very valuable for one question and limited for another; evaluation must explain how origin, purpose and content affect its value for the specific investigation.'),
       ('Explain why oral history interviews can be both valuable and limited.', 'They provide personal experiences unavailable elsewhere but are affected by memory, hindsight, the interviewer’s questions and the interviewee’s purposes.'),
       ('Explain why a historian should use a range of sources.', 'A range of perspectives allows corroboration, reveals bias and builds a fuller picture.')],
  contrast=[('authenticity', 'reliability', 'Authenticity concerns whether a source is genuine; reliability concerns whether a genuine source’s content can be trusted for a purpose. A genuine source can still be unreliable.')],
  misc=[('A source is useless if it is biased.', 'Bias may make a source valuable as evidence of attitudes and perspectives.'),
        ('Websites should never be used in a historical investigation.', 'Websites can be used if their origin is credible and they are evaluated critically, e.g. digitised archives or academic sites.')],
  cases=[('the Hitler Diaries hoax of 1983', 'Stern paid millions of marks for 60 volumes; forensic tests found that the paper and glue contained post-war whiteners.',
          'the importance of verifying authenticity', 'It showed that even eminent historians and editors can be deceived without forensic and provenance checks.')],
  src=[('Tests showed that the paper contained a whitening agent not used in paper until the 1950s, and the bindings contained polyester threads. The diaries are a crude forgery.',
        'Paraphrased from the report of the West German Federal Archives (Bundesarchiv) on the “Hitler Diaries”, 6 May 1983',
        'The diaries were forgeries because forensic tests found materials made after the war.',
        'An official scientific examination by the national archives, giving conclusive evidence about authenticity.',
        'It addresses only authenticity and does not explain how the forgery was produced or why experts were deceived.'),
       ('I am satisfied that the documents are authentic; and that the standard accounts of Hitler’s behaviour will have to be revised.',
        'Paraphrased from Hugh Trevor-Roper, British historian, article in The Times, 23 April 1983',
        'The diaries are authentic and will change historians’ understanding of Hitler.',
        'Written by an eminent historian of Hitler, it shows how the forgery initially convinced experts.',
        'Trevor-Roper had little time to examine the diaries and did not read German handwriting well; he later withdrew his judgement.')],
  cmp='Both give an expert judgement on the authenticity of the Hitler Diaries. The Bundesarchiv concludes on scientific evidence that they are forgeries, while Trevor-Roper declares them authentic; the archive relies on forensic testing, the historian on a quick examination of content.',
  ev=[('Primary sources are always more useful than secondary sources in a historical investigation.',
       ['direct evidence of the past', 'enable original analysis', 'show contemporary perspectives'],
       ['secondary sources provide context and interpretations', 'primary sources may be forged or biased', 'usefulness depends on the question'],
       'Both are essential; their usefulness depends on the research question and evaluation.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the value and limitations of oral history for a historical investigation on the Rwandan genocide.',
           ['survivor and perpetrator testimony', 'memory and trauma', 'political pressures in Rwanda', 'corroboration'],
           'Oral history is invaluable for experience but must be corroborated and evaluated carefully.')],
)

H('ch14-st3', 'organising an argument and reflecting on the methods of historians',
  terms=[('reflection', 'the part of the investigation in which the student considers what the process revealed about the methods and challenges of historians'),
         ('analysis', 'breaking down evidence and arguments to show how they support or challenge a judgement'),
         ('selection of evidence', 'the historian’s choice of which evidence to use, which shapes the argument')],
  facts=[('Who wrote “The Return of Martin Guerre” (1983), reflecting on the role of invention in historical reconstruction?', 'Natalie Zemon Davis', ['Richard Evans', 'E. H. Carr', 'Pierre Nora'], 'Davis described her reconstruction as partly invention, held in check by the voices of the past.'),
         ('Which historian wrote “In Defence of History” (1997), responding to postmodernist critiques?', 'Richard J. Evans', ['Keith Jenkins', 'Hayden White', 'Michel Foucault'], 'Evans argued that historians can achieve reliable knowledge.')],
  why=[('Explain why reflection is an important part of the historical investigation.', 'It shows understanding of how historians work – selecting evidence, dealing with bias and gaps, making interpretations – and links the student’s experience to historical method.'),
       ('Explain why the investigation should be organised around an argument rather than chronology.', 'An argument-based structure keeps the focus on the research question and builds a judgement, whereas chronology tends to produce narrative.')],
  contrast=[('the investigation section', 'the reflection section', 'The investigation presents an argument answering the research question using evidence; the reflection considers what the process revealed about the methods and challenges of historians.')],
  misc=[('The reflection should summarise the investigation’s findings.', 'Reflection should focus on the methods and challenges of history, linked to the investigation, not repeat the conclusion.'),
        ('A good investigation avoids discussing gaps in the evidence.', 'Acknowledging gaps and uncertainty shows critical understanding and strengthens the judgement.')],
  cases=[('Natalie Zemon Davis’s work on the sixteenth-century impostor Arnaud du Tilh', 'Davis combined court records with informed speculation to reconstruct the lives of peasants in a famous case of identity fraud.',
          'the challenges historians face with limited evidence', 'It shows how historians must use imagination carefully when evidence is incomplete, and be transparent about it.')],
  src=[('What I offer you here is in part my invention, but held tightly in check by the voices of the past.',
        'Natalie Zemon Davis, US historian, The Return of Martin Guerre, 1983 (extract)',
        'Historical reconstruction involves some invention, but it is controlled by evidence from the past.',
        'An honest reflection by a respected historian on her own methods.',
        'It concerns a single, exceptionally documented case; it may not apply to all historical work.'),
       ('History can be true, if not absolutely so. The past did happen, and historians can, with care and honesty, reach reliable, if provisional, conclusions about it.',
        'Paraphrased from Richard J. Evans, British historian, In Defence of History, 1997',
        'Historians can reach reliable, though not absolute, truths about the past if they work carefully.',
        'A considered defence of historical method by a leading historian.',
        'It was written as a polemic against postmodernist theorists, so it may simplify their views.')],
  cmp='Both discuss the limits of historical knowledge. Davis admits that her reconstruction is partly invented but controlled by evidence, while Evans insists that historians can reach reliable, though provisional, truths; both accept limits, but Davis emphasises interpretation and Evans reliability.',
  ev=[('Historians can never know the past as it really was.',
       ['incomplete evidence', 'selection and interpretation', 'Davis’s “invention”'],
       ['evidence constrains interpretation', 'corroboration and method', 'Evans: provisional truth'],
       'Absolute knowledge is impossible, but disciplined method yields reliable conclusions.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the challenges facing historians when investigating events for which evidence is limited.',
           ['gaps and silences', 'bias and perspective', 'use of inference', 'examples: Rwanda local records, Davis'],
           'Limited evidence requires transparent inference and careful corroboration.')],
)

H('ch14-st4', 'academic honesty and the use of sources in historical writing',
  terms=[('plagiarism', 'presenting the words, ideas or work of another person as one’s own without acknowledgement'),
         ('bibliography', 'an alphabetical list of all sources consulted, following a consistent referencing style'),
         ('citation', 'a reference in the text or footnotes identifying the source of a quotation, idea or piece of information')],
  facts=[('Which of the following must be cited in a historical investigation?', 'Paraphrased ideas taken from a historian’s book', ['The student’s own conclusions', 'Common knowledge such as the date of the end of the Second World War', 'The research question'], 'Paraphrased ideas still belong to the original author.'),
         ('In the libel case brought by David Irving against Deborah Lipstadt (2000), what did the judge conclude?', 'Irving had deliberately misrepresented and manipulated historical evidence', ['Lipstadt had defamed Irving', 'Irving was a reliable historian', 'The case should be dismissed without judgement'], 'Mr Justice Gray found Irving was a Holocaust denier who distorted evidence.')],
  why=[('Explain why accurate referencing is important in historical writing.', 'It allows readers to check evidence, credits other historians and distinguishes the writer’s own argument from others’ work.'),
       ('Explain why the Irving v. Lipstadt case is significant for historians.', 'It showed in court that deliberate manipulation of sources could be exposed by checking references, defending the integrity of historical method.')],
  contrast=[('a footnote', 'a bibliography', 'A footnote identifies the specific source for a particular point; a bibliography lists all sources consulted, usually alphabetically, at the end.')],
  misc=[('Paraphrasing a source in your own words means you do not need to cite it.', 'The ideas still belong to the original author and must be cited.'),
        ('Using AI tools to write the investigation is acceptable as long as you check the facts.', 'Work generated by others, including AI tools, must not be presented as one’s own; any permitted use must be acknowledged according to IB academic integrity policy.')],
  cases=[('the plagiarism controversy surrounding Stephen Ambrose in 2002', 'The popular US historian was found to have copied passages from other authors in “The Wild Blue” and other books, with only footnote acknowledgement and no quotation marks.',
          'the importance of academic honesty', 'It shows that copying wording without quotation marks is plagiarism even when a source is mentioned.')],
  src=[('Irving has for his own ideological reasons persistently and deliberately misrepresented and manipulated historical evidence; he is an active Holocaust denier.',
        'Mr Justice Charles Gray, judgment in Irving v. Penguin Books and Deborah Lipstadt, High Court, London, 11 April 2000 (adapted)',
        'Irving deliberately misrepresented and manipulated historical evidence for ideological reasons and is a Holocaust denier.',
        'A legal judgment based on detailed expert analysis (including by Richard Evans) of Irving’s use of sources.',
        'It is a legal judgment on a libel claim, not a historical study, and addresses only the specific issues in the case.'),
       ('Students must understand that copying wording, even with a footnote, without quotation marks is plagiarism. Every idea taken from another writer must be acknowledged.',
        'Paraphrased from a university history department’s academic integrity guidance for students (illustrative)',
        'All borrowed wording must be quoted and all borrowed ideas acknowledged, otherwise it is plagiarism.',
        'It shows the standards expected in academic historical writing.',
        'It is general guidance, not specific to IB requirements, and does not discuss particular cases.')],
  cmp='Both concern the honest use of evidence. The Gray judgment exposes deliberate manipulation of sources by a writer, while the guidance sets rules for acknowledging the work of others; one concerns distortion of evidence, the other plagiarism.',
  ev=[('Academic honesty is as important as historical knowledge in the work of historians.',
       ['credibility depends on honest use of evidence', 'Irving case', 'Ambrose plagiarism'],
       ['knowledge and interpretation are the substance of history', 'honesty is a minimum requirement rather than a skill'],
       'Honesty is a precondition of valid history; without it, knowledge cannot be trusted.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the importance of referencing and academic honesty for the credibility of historical writing.',
           ['checking sources', 'Irving v. Lipstadt', 'plagiarism cases', 'IB academic integrity'],
           'Referencing makes history verifiable, and verifiability is the basis of its credibility.')],
)
